   "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Julian_(emperor)",
   "title": "Julian (emperor) - Wikipedia",
   "snippet": "<strong>Julian</strong> (Latin: Flavius Claudius Julianus; Ancient Greek: Ἰουλιανός Ioulianos; 331 – 26 June 363) was Caesar of Western Rome from 355 to 360 and Roman emperor from 361 to 363, as well as a notable philosopher and author in Greek. His rejection of Christianity, public conversion to ...",
   "text": "Julian (Flavius Claudius Julianus) was born in Constantinople in 331, son of Julius Constantius and Basilina, and died 26 June 363 near Samarra in Mesopotamia, aged 31–32; he was buried at Tarsus, later translated to the Church of the Holy Apostles. He married Helena (m. 355, d. 360). He was Caesar in the West from 6 November 355, was proclaimed Augustus by his troops in February 360, and reigned as sole emperor from 3 November 361 until his death, succeeded by Jovian.\n\nHis material circumstances in youth were shaped by violence and confinement. The Wikipedia page reports that after Constantine's death in 337, Julian's cousin Constantius II 'appears to have led' a massacre of most of Julian's close relatives — descendants of the second marriage of Constantius Chlorus and Theodora — leaving only Constantius II, Constantine II, Constans, and the cousins Julian and Gallus; the page hedges that Julian and Gallus 'were likely saved by their youth.' The two boys were excluded from public life, strictly guarded, and given a Christian education. Julian's conversion to paganism came around age 20; looking back in 362, the page reports, Julian wrote that he had spent twenty years in the way of Christianity and twelve in the true way, that of Helios. He began Neoplatonic study in Asia Minor in 351 under Aedesius, then Eusebius of Myndus. The page, citing the historian Eunapius, recounts that Eusebius described Maximus of Ephesus making a statue of Hecate smile and her torches ignite, while warning Julian against the 'impostures of witchcraft and magic that cheat the senses'; Julian nonetheless sought out Maximus, telling Eusebius on leaving, per Eunapius: 'farewell, and devote yourself to your books. You have shown me the man I was in search of.'\n\nAfter Gallus, made Caesar of the East in 351, was executed in 354 for a rule of terror, Julian was summoned to Constantius' court at Mediolanum and held for a year under suspicion of treasonable intrigue; the page reports he was cleared partly through Empress Eusebia's intervention and permitted to study in Athens, where he knew Gregory of Nazianzus and Basil the Great and was initiated into the Eleusinian Mysteries. (A cited note attributes to Shaun Tougher the argument that the kind Eusebia of Julian's panegyric is a literary creation and that she was doing Constantius' bidding.)\n\nAs Caesar in Gaul, Julian's material conditions were those of a cash-strapped frontier command. At the end of 357, the page reports, he prevented a tax increase by the Gallic praetorian prefect Florentius and personally took charge of Belgica Secunda, his first civil administration; he clashed repeatedly with Florentius over tax increases and corruption, judging that rebuilding devastated cities and winning the civil population's support required resisting new exactions. In February 360 Constantius ordered more than half of Julian's Gallic troops east after Shapur II took Amida following a 73-day siege; the order bypassed Julian and went directly to his commanders, provoking an insurrection of the Petulantes, who refused to leave Gaul. The page reports Zosimus' claim that army officers distributed an anonymous tract complaining against Constantius and fearing for Julian's fate, notes that Florentius was notably absent organizing supplies at Vienne, and that Ammianus Marcellinus suggested Constantius sent the order out of fear of Julian's popularity, at Florentius' urging. In spring 361 Julian captured the Alamannic king Vadomarius, claiming Vadomarius had been in league with Constantius, then divided his forces and moved down the Danube; the page quotes Julian's statement of late November that he marched 'because, having been declared a public enemy, I meant to frighten him [Constantius] merely, and that our quarrel should result in intercourse on more friendly terms...'\n\nAs emperor, the page reports, Julian blamed Constantine for the state of the administration, idealized Hadrian and Marcus Aurelius, and in his first panegyric to Constantius described the ideal ruler as primus inter pares ('first among equals'), operating under the same laws as his subjects; in Constantinople he frequently attended the Senate, debating and speaking at the level of other members.\n\nMaterial constraints shaped his religious policy. The page reports that communal sacrifice-and-feasting festivals had collapsed, that civic leaders lacked funds and support to hold them, and that Julian found the sacred temple funds that had financed them had been seized by his uncle Constantine to support the Christian Church; leading politicians therefore preferred religiously neutral ceremonies. Christian charities, open to all including pagans, had taken this part of citizens' lives out of imperial control, so Julian envisioned a Roman philanthropic system and regulated pagan priests' conduct; the page quotes him: 'These impious Galileans not only feed their own poor, but ours also; welcoming them into their agapae, they attract them, as children are attracted, with cakes.' His edict of 4 February 362 proclaimed all religions equal before the law; the page notes it was seen as favor toward the Jews intended to upset Christians. His School Edict required imperial approval of public teachers, whose salaries the state paid or supplemented; the page reports Ammianus Marcellinus' explanation that this aimed to bar Christian teachers from pagan texts, and quotes the edict: 'If they want to learn literature, they have Luke and Mark: Let them go back to their churches and expound on them' — noting the edict dealt a severe financial blow to Christian teachers by depriving them of students. At Delphi, the page reports, Julian ordered the bones of bishop Babylas removed from Apollo's temple after being advised they suppressed the god, a 'public-relations mistake' that produced a massive Christian procession; when the temple burned soon after, he suspected Christians, ordered stricter investigations, and shut the city's chief Christian church before investigations proved the fire accidental. The page reports that the failure to rebuild the Jerusalem Temple may be ascribed to the Galilee earthquake of 363; Gregory Nazianzen described builders driven together by wind and earth-heaving and a flame issuing from a church, adding this is 'what all people nowadays report and believe,' while the page notes Gibbon in the 18th century considered this unreliable, positing sabotage or accident, and that divine intervention is a common view among Christian historians.\n\nFor the Persian campaign, the page reports that on 5 March 363, despite omens against it, Julian left Antioch with about 65,000–83,000 men, or 80,000–90,000 (the traditional figure accepted by Gibbon being 95,000), refused offers of assistance from small powers en route but ordered the Armenian king Arsaces to muster an army, crossed the Euphrates near Hierapolis, and sent 30,000 men under Procopius and Sebastianus to devastate Media with the Armenians. He had built a fleet of over 1,000 ships at Samosata to supply a march down the Euphrates, plus 50 pontoon ships; his ultimate aim, the page says it 'seems,' was regime change, replacing Shapur II with his brother Hormisdas. Turning south at Circesium in early April, passing Dura on 6 April, the army captured the fortress of Pirisabora at the end of April; the Sassanids broke the dikes, turning the land to marsh and slowing the advance. By mid-May the Romans won a tactical victory before Ctesiphon, but the page reports that master-general Victor ordered troops not to enter the open gates for fear of encirclement; the council of war persuaded Julian not to besiege the city, and he marched east into the interior, ordering the fleet destroyed — a 'hasty decision,' the page calls it, leaving him on the wrong side of the Tigris with inadequate siege equipment, the Persians burning food in his path and flooding the area behind him. A second council on 16 June 363 decided to withdraw northward to Corduene. The page labels apocryphal the report that his dying words were νενίκηκάς με, Γαλιλαῖε ('You have won, Galilean'), attributing it to Theodoret's Historia ecclesiastica in the 5th century.\n\nThe page also records Julian's own cosmological claims, quoting him: 'when Zeus was setting all things in order there fell from him drops of sacred blood, and from them, as they say, arose the race of men,' and his argument against a single-pair origin of humanity, 'how very different in their bodies are the Germans and Scythians from the Libyans and Ethiopians.' The Christian historian Socrates Scholasticus claims Julian believed himself Alexander the Great 'in another body' via transmigration of souls. The page describes Julian as a philosopher and author in Greek, remembered in Christian tradition as Julian the Apostate for his rejection of Christianity and promotion of Neoplatonic Hellenism."
  },
  {
   "url": "https://www.newworldencyclopedia.org/entry/Julian_the_Apostate",
   "title": "Julian the Apostate - New World Encyclopedia",
   "snippet": "Others (Rowland Smith, in particular) have argued that Julian&#x27;s philosophical perspective was nothing unusual for a &quot;cultured&quot; Pagan of his time, and, at any rate, that Julian&#x27;s Paganism was not limited to philosophy alone, and that he was deeply devoted to the same Gods and Goddesses as other Pagans of his day. According to Christian historian Socrates Scholasticus (iii, 21), Julian believed himself to be Alexander the Great in another body via transmigration of souls, as taught by Plato and Pythagoras.",
   "text": "This New World Encyclopedia reference page (itself a rewrite of a Wikipedia article, per its own closing notice) supplies the following on Julian's material circumstances. Born in Constantinople in 331, Julian was a child when, in the turmoil after Constantine's death in 337, his cousin Constantius II ordered the massacre of much of Julian's family; the page reports that Constantius then imposed a strict Arian Christian education on the surviving Julian and his half-brother Gallus. Julian grew up initially in Bithynia at his maternal grandmother's, tutored from age seven by Eusebius, Bishop of Nicomedia, and Mardonius, a Gothic eunuch; in 342 he and Gallus were exiled to the imperial estate of Macellum in Cappadocia, the exile lifted when he was eighteen. In 354 Gallus was executed and Julian briefly imprisoned; on November 6, 355 Constantius summoned him to Milan, made him Caesar of the West, and married him to his sister Helena. As Caesar in Gaul, Julian campaigned with limited forces — the page gives 13,000 men for his victory over the Alamanni at Strasbourg — and on the civil side prevented a tax increase by the Gallic praetorian prefect Florentius and personally administered Belgica Secunda. Proclaimed emperor by his troops in Paris in 360 after Constantius ordered Gallic troops east, he avoided civil war only through Constantius's death; the page notes Aquileia was besieged by 23,000 of his loyalists. As sole emperor he cut imperial court expenses, removed eunuchs from office, and reduced servants and guard, while also setting up the Chalcedon tribunal under which some of Constantius's followers were tortured and killed under Arbitio's supervision. Financially his religious policy worked through property and privilege: the page reports he forced the Church to return riches looted from pagan temples after Constantine (or fines equal to them), ordered lands taken by the Church restored to their original owners, stripped bishops of free state travel, and in the Tolerance Edict of 362 decreed reopening of temples and restitution of alienated temple property. His School Edict, which the page quotes directly — Julian wrote that if Christians wanted literature they had Luke and Mark and should go back to their churches and expound on them — is described as also a severe financial blow, depriving Christian teachers of students. On charity, the page quotes two passages attributed to Julian (cited to his Epistulae and to secondary sources): that the 'impious Galileans' feed not only their own poor but his, attracting people with cakes at their agapae, and that while pagan priests neglect the poor the Galileans' display of 'false compassion' spreads their errors and causes contempt for the gods. The page, summarizing secondary authors (Uhlhorn, Lecky, Woods), says Julian envisioned a state-run Roman philanthropic system and a pagan hierarchy precisely because Christian charity had placed citizens' welfare outside imperial control, and that pagan philanthropy had been 'dictated much more by policy than by benevolence' (Lecky's phrase as quoted). For the Persian campaign the page gives about 90,000 men advancing from Antioch with 30,000 detached under Procopius toward Armenia; Julian died of a spear wound near Maranga on June 26, 363, aged 31, treated by his physician Oribasius with wound irrigation and attempted intestinal suturing. The page hedges that Libanius's claim Julian was assassinated by a Christian soldier of his own army is uncorroborated by Ammianus Marcellinus or other contemporaries, and that the dying words 'Vicisti, Galilaee' are considered apocryphal."
  }
