  {
   "url": "https://www.encyclopedia.com/people/history/ancient-history-late-roman-and-byzantine-biographies/julian-apostate",
   "title": "Julian The Apostate | Encyclopedia.com",
   "snippet": "Julian the Apostate (Flavius Claudius Julianus), 331?–363, Roman emperor (361–63), nephew of Constantine I; successor of Constantius II. He was given an education that combined Christian and Neoplatonic ideas. He and his half-brother Gallus were sent (c.341) to Cappadocia.",
   "text": "The New Catholic Encyclopedia entry (by R. H. Schmandt, summarised on Encyclopedia.com) gives Julian's dates as born c. 331, died June 26, 363, Roman emperor 361–363. He was the son of Julius Constantius, half-brother of Constantine the Great; his mother Basilina died shortly after his birth, and his father perished in 337 in the slaughter that brought the illegitimate branch of Constantine's descendants to power. The entry stresses that these fraternal rivalries shaped Julian's whole life: with his brother Gallus he lived, in the entry's words, precariously and in obscurity, chiefly at Macellum in Asia Minor. His tutor Mardonius, described as probably a pagan, introduced him to Hellenistic culture; at Macellum he also read Christian literature extensively, received baptism, and even served as lector in church, though most clergy he knew, such as Eusebius of Nicomedia and George of Cappadocia, were Arians. When Gallus became Caesar in 351, Julian was permitted to travel and study; Libanius and others deepened his love of Greek culture, and contact with the theurgist Neoplatonist Maximus of Ephesus led to his secret apostasy from Christianity c. 351 and initiation into the cult of Mithra. The fall of Gallus in 354 endangered him: Constantius II summoned him west, where he lived under surveillance. In 355 Constantius, having no sons, appointed him Caesar with jurisdiction over the West; in 360 his soldiers proclaimed him Augustus in a mutiny, and only Constantius's death in 361, with civil war imminent, left him accepted as sole emperor. The entry characterises his standing as emperor as constrained by his own priorities: except for minor administrative measures, he concerned himself with religion and the Persian war in which he died. Disinclined to persecution by force, he proclaimed toleration for all Christian sects while revoking Christian privileges, removing Christians from political office, and forbidding them to teach the classical curriculum; he exiled St. Athanasius and refused to defend Nisibis because of its Christian population. His religious program met, the entry says, with complete apathy. The Oxford Dictionary of Phrase and Fable entry (by Elizabeth Knowles) dates his reign from 360, says he restored paganism as the state cult in place of Christianity, reversed after his death on the Persian campaign, and reports — with the hedge 'are said to have been' — that his last words were, vicisti, Galilaee ('you have conquered, Galilean')."
  },
