   "text": "The Wikipedia reference page on Julian (emperor) records the institutional framework of his life and reign. Born in Constantinople in 331, full name Flavius Claudius Julianus, he was Caesar of the West from 6 November 355 and Augustus from 3 November 361 (having been proclaimed by his troops in February 360) until his death on 26 June 363; he succeeded Constantius II and was succeeded by Jovian. The page reports that after Constantine's death in 337, Constantius II appears to have led a massacre of Julian's close relatives, and that Julian and his half-brother Gallus were excluded from public life, strictly guarded, and given a Christian education — the page hedges that they were 'likely' saved by their youth, and that Constantius's later guilt rests on 'if Julian's later writings are to be believed.'\n\nOn religious and philosophical institutions: the page reports that Julian began studying Neoplatonism in Asia Minor in 351 under Aedesius and then Eusebius of Myndus, and through Eusebius's account of Maximus of Ephesus sought Maximus out as mentor. According to the historian Eunapius, as the page relays it, Julian on leaving Eusebius said in substance (paraphrase) that Eusebius should keep to his books, since he had shown him the man he was seeking. In Athens, where he was permitted to study after being cleared of treason suspicion in 354 — cleared partly, the page notes, because Empress Eusebia intervened, though the page also cites Shaun Tougher's argument that the kindly Eusebia of Julian's panegyric may be a literary creation acting at Constantius's behest — Julian was initiated into the Eleusinian Mysteries, which he later tried to restore. Looking back in 362, the page reports, Julian wrote (paraphrase) that he had spent twenty years in the Christian way and twelve in the true way of Helios.\n\nOn civil administration: the page records that at the end of 357 Julian blocked a tax increase by the Gallic praetorian prefect Florentius and personally took charge of Belgica Secunda, his first civil-administration role, one properly belonging to the prefect; the two clashed repeatedly over taxation and Florentius's corruption. In February 360 Constantius II ordered more than half of Julian's Gallic troops east, bypassing Julian; the page reports Zosimus's claim that officers distributed an anonymous tract against Constantius, notes Florentius's conspicuous absence at Vienne, and reports Ammianus Marcellinus's suggestion that Constantius sent the order out of fear of Julian's popularity, urged by Florentius. After his acclamation and advance in 361, the page preserves Julian's own statement of late November (paraphrase): that having been declared a public enemy he meant merely to frighten Constantius so their quarrel might end in friendlier intercourse.\n\nAs emperor, the page reports, Julian rejected his predecessors' administrative style, blamed Constantine for the state of the administration, restored neither the tetrarchy nor absolute autocracy, and idealized Hadrian and Marcus Aurelius; in his first panegyric to Constantius he described the ideal ruler as essentially primus inter pares, subject to the same laws as his subjects, and in Constantinople he frequently attended the Senate, debating and speaking as one member among others.\n\nOn religious policy as institutional action: at Delphi, advised that the bones of bishop Babylas were suppressing the oracle, he ordered their removal — which the page calls a public-relations mistake that drew a massive Christian procession; when the temple then burned, he suspected Christians, closed the city's chief Christian church, and ordered investigations that proved the fire accidental. On 4 February 362 he promulgated an edict guaranteeing freedom of religion, declaring all religions equal before the law; the page reports it was seen as favoring the Jews to upset Christians. His School Edict required imperial approval of all public teachers, with the state paying or supplementing salaries; Ammianus Marcellinus, as the page relays, explains the aim as barring Christian teachers from pagan texts, and the page quotes the edict's sense (paraphrase) that Christians who want literature have Luke and Mark and should expound them in their churches — the page flags 'citation needed' on parts of this account and notes the edict dealt a severe financial blow to Christian teachers. The page reports that temple funds had been seized by Constantine to support the Church, that civic leaders lacked funds and motive to revive festivals, and that Julian, observing Christian charity's reach, envisioned a Roman philanthropic system and regulated pagan priests' conduct; it preserves his complaint (paraphrase) that the 'impious Galileans' feed not only their own poor but his, attracting them with cakes at their agapae. On the failed Temple rebuilding in Jerusalem, the page reports Gregory Nazianzen's contemporary account of earth-heaving and flame, notes Gregory's hedge that this is what all people now report and believe, and records that Gibbon preferred sabotage or accident. The page also relays Socrates Scholasticus's claim that Julian believed himself Alexander the Great in another body, and quotes Julian's arguments against a single-pair origin of humanity (paraphrase: that Zeus's drops of sacred blood produced the race of men, and that Germans and Scythians differ so in body from Libyans and Ethiopians). His dying words 'You have won, Galilean' are labeled apocryphal, from Theodoret's 5th-century Historia ecclesiastica."
  },
  {
   "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Julian_(emperor)",
   "title": "Julian (emperor) - Wikipedia",
   "snippet": "<strong>Julian</strong> (Latin: Flavius Claudius ... Roman <strong>emperor</strong> from 361 to 363, as well as a notable philosopher and author in Greek. His rejection of Christianity, public conversion to paganism, and promotion of Neoplatonic Hellenism caused him to be remembered as <strong>Julian</strong> <strong>the</strong> <strong>Apostate</strong> in the Christian ...",
   "text": "Julian (Flavius Claudius Julianus, born Constantinople 331, died 26 June 363 near Samarra) was Caesar in the West from 6 November 355 and Augustus from 3 November 361 (proclaimed by his troops in February 360), succeeding Constantius II and succeeded by Jovian; his religion is listed as Hellenic paganism, previously Nicene Christianity. On institutional matters, the Wikipedia page reports the following.\n\nAfter the massacre of his relatives in 337 — which the page says Constantius II \"appears to have led\" and \"allegedly ordered,\" adding the hedge that Julian and his half-brother Gallus \"were likely saved by their youth\" — Julian and Gallus were excluded from public life, strictly guarded, and given a Christian education. In 354, after Gallus was executed for a rule of terror, Julian was summoned to Constantius' court at Mediolanum and held for a year under suspicion of treasonable intrigue; he was cleared partly through Empress Eusebia's intervention and permitted to study in Athens, though the page's footnote records Shaun Tougher's argument that the kindly Eusebia of Julian's panegyric is a literary creation and that she was doing her husband's bidding.\n\nAs Caesar in Gaul, Julian clashed repeatedly with the praetorian prefect Florentius over the administration of the province: at the end of 357 he prevented a tax increase Florentius supported and personally took charge of Belgica Secunda, his first experience of civil administration, though the page notes this role properly belonged to the praetorian prefect. When Constantius' order of February 360 transferring more than half of Julian's Gallic troops provoked the insurrection that led to his acclamation, Julian would later blame Florentius for the order's arrival, and Ammianus Marcellinus even suggested Constantius sent it out of fear of Julian's popularity, on Florentius' urging.\n\nAs emperor, the page reports, Julian rejected his predecessors' style of administration, blamed Constantine for its state and for abandoning older traditions, made no attempt to restore Diocletian's tetrarchy, and did not rule as an absolute autocrat; he idealized Hadrian and Marcus Aurelius. In his first panegyric to Constantius he described the ideal ruler as essentially primus inter pares (\"first among equals\"), operating under the same laws as his subjects, and in Constantinople he was frequently active in the Senate, participating in debates and making speeches at the level of other members. In late November 361 he had stated, in a letter the page quotes, that he marched on Constantius \"because, having been declared a public enemy, I meant to frighten him [Constantius] merely, and that our quarrel should result in intercourse on more friendly terms...\"\n\nOn religious institutions: on 4 February 362 Julian promulgated an edict guaranteeing freedom of religion, proclaiming all religions equal before the law and returning the Empire to religious eclecticism; the page reports the edict \"was seen as an act of favor toward the Jews, in order to upset the Christians.\" His School Edict required all public teachers to be approved by the Emperor, with the state paying or supplementing much of their salaries; Ammianus Marcellinus explains this as intending to prevent Christian teachers from using pagan texts such as the Iliad, and the page quotes the edict: \"If they want to learn literature, they have Luke and Mark: Let them go back to their churches and expound on them.\" The page adds (flagged citation needed) that this aimed to remove Christian schools' influence and dealt a severe financial blow to Christian teachers by depriving them of students. Julian found that the sacred temple funds which had financed pagan festivals had been seized by his uncle Constantine to support the Christian Church, and that civic leaders lacked funds and motivation to revive festivals, preferring religiously neutral ceremonies. Because Christian charities were open to all, including pagans, that part of citizens' lives lay outside Imperial authority, so Julian envisioned a Roman philanthropic system and concerned himself with the morality of pagan priests, writing (as the page quotes): \"These impious Galileans not only feed their own poor, but ours also; welcoming them into their agapae, they attract them, as children are attracted, with cakes.\" At Antioch, advised that the bones of the 3rd-century bishop Babylas were suppressing the oracle of Apollo, he ordered their removal — which the page calls a public-relations mistake that produced a massive Christian procession — and when the temple then burned, he suspected Christians, ordered stricter investigations than usual, and shut the city's chief Christian church before the fire proved accidental. On his attempt to rebuild the Jerusalem Temple, the page reports that failure may be ascribed to the Galilee earthquake of 363; it quotes Gregory Nazianzen's Orations describing the builders driven apart by wind and earth-heaving and a flame issuing from a church, which Gregory calls \"what all people nowadays report and believe,\" and notes that Gibbon considered this unreliable, positing sabotage or accident, while divine intervention is a common view among Christian historians. The report that his dying words were \"You have won, Galilean\" is described as apocryphal, from Theodoret's Historia ecclesiastica in the 5th century."
  },
  {
   "url": "https://jewishvirtuallibrary.org/julian-the-apostate-x00b0",
   "title": "Julian the Apostate°",
   "snippet": "Julian saw Christianity – which within a generation had ceased to be a persecuted belief and had become the official religion persecuting others – as a sickness within the body politic, and felt deep revulsion toward it from an ethical-religious viewpoint. Although he issued an edict of universal religious toleration, he gave practical expression to his opposition to Christianity by founding a pagan cult in which he served as pontifex maximus.",
   "text": "Julian the Apostate°\n\nLibrary Wings\n\nAbout\n\nBookstore\n\nGlossary\n\nLinks\n\nPublications\n\nTimelines\n\nVirtual Tours\n\nDONATE SUBSCRIBE\n\nAbout\n\nBookstore\n\nGlossary\n\nLinks\n\nNews\n\nPublications\n\nTimelines\n\nThe Virtual Jewish World\n\nVirtual Israel Experience\n\nContact\n\nPrivacy Policy\n\nDonate\n\nSign Up to Stay Informed\n\nSubscribe Donate\n\nThe Jewish Virtual Library is a project of the American-Israeli Cooperative Enterprise (AICE), a 501(c)(3) nonprofit, nonpartisan educational organization. | © 1998–2026 American-Israeli Cooperative Enterprise\n\nThe Jewish Virtual Library is a free educational resource. This site may display limited advertising to help support operations. Advertising is not the primary purpose of this site. This site includes links to external third-party resources that JVL's editorial team has selected for their educational value."
