  {
   "url": "https://roman-emperors.sites.luc.edu/julian.htm",
   "title": "Roman Emperors - DIR Julian the Apostate",
   "snippet": "As was suggested earlier, a goal ... social institutions. The earlier letter to Alypius and Mamertinus&#x27; panegyric established how Julian wished various societal elites to function as intercessors between himself and the broader society at large. Julian wished for his religious officials to serve in this same ...",
   "text": "This De Imperatoribus Romanis entry on Julian (360–363 A.D.) is a secondary summary that leans heavily on Ammianus Marcellinus, Julian's own letters, and epigraphic evidence; where it records Julian's own words I paraphrase rather than quote, since the page quotes him and I will not risk corrupting a quotation.\n\nOn Julian and the imperial institution: the page reports that Constantius II made Julian Caesar on 6 November 355 because, in the page's framing of a truth evident since the Tetrarchy, the empire was too big for one man, and an imperial presence was needed in Gaul; Julian was, after the earlier purges, the only viable imperial-family candidate. A few days after his elevation he was married to Constantius' sister Helena to cement the alliance. The page cites Ammianus for Julian being an able administrator who corrected the injustices of Constantius' appointees — Ammianus relates that Julian blocked Florentius, Praetorian Prefect of Gaul, from raising taxes, and that Julian personally took over the governorship of Belgica Secunda. Hilary, bishop of Poitiers, is cited as supporting that assessment, reporting Julian was an able representative of the emperor to the Gallic provincials. Epigraphic evidence is offered for his popularity with provincial elites: an inscription found near Beneventum dedicates to him as, in the page's quoted Latin-to-English rendering, most noble and sanctified Caesar, from Tocius Maximus, vir clarissimus — a man at the top of the social spectrum, which the page reads as showing Julian successfully cultivated a positive image among local elites.\n\nOn the army and accession: the page notes a near-mutiny when soldiers restoring forts in the Mosa region were on short rations and had received no donative since Julian became Caesar; he soothed them. Ammianus' story is relayed of Julian, agonizing over the soldiers' acclamation, dreaming that the Genius of the Roman state warned him this was his last chance at power. The page reports that Julian himself, in a November 361 letter to Maximus of Ephesus, claimed the soldiers proclaimed him Augustus against his will but defended the accession as willed by the gods, saying he had treated enemies with clemency and had led the troops in propitiating the traditional deities because the gods commanded a return to the old rites (paraphrase of the letter as the page summarizes it). Constantius died of natural causes on 3 November 361 near Mopsucrenae in Cilicia, naming Julian — the page hedges, 'the sources say' — as legitimate successor. Julian reached Constantinople on 11 December 361, gave Constantius full funeral rites, then trimmed the overstaffed imperial bureaucracy, expelling the proliferated cooks and barbers of the previous reign. The page reports Ammianus gave a mixed assessment of Julian's treatment of Constantius' followers: against the tradition of clemency, Julian gave some men over to death to appease the army.\n\nOn the consulate and municipal institutions: the page treats the consulate as a concrete bond between a divinely mandated emperor and the recipient, noting that Mamertinus, named consul, went on to hold the Prefecture of Italy, Illyricum, and Africa under Julian, and that the municipal senate of Aceruntia in Apulia erected a monument styling Julian, in the page's rendering, 'Repairer of the World.'\n\nOn religious institutions: the page notes Julian's nominally Christian upbringing (debate over his mother Basilina's Christianity is flagged as resting on 'rather flimsy' evidence) and reports that in a 363 letter to the Alexandrians Julian stated he abandoned Christianity at twenty and had followed the traditional gods for the twelve years since (paraphrase). As emperor he initially lifted the ban on Arians and other sects — writing to the Anomean bishop Aetius inviting him back without fear of reprisal — but the page reports Ammianus' claim that this toleration was a ruse to let Christian factions destroy each other. The page then describes a threefold anti-Christian strategy: legislative exclusion, a rival pagan church structure, and philosophical attack. Specifics given: a Theodosian Code law barring decurions from evading compulsory duties on grounds of being Christian; Ammianus' report of legislation barring Christians from teaching rhetoric and grammar, with Julian's rescript (Ep. 36.423.A-D) arguing Christians who taught the classics while ridiculing the ancestral beliefs were impious (paraphrase); a law of 405 upholding Julian's ban on the Donatists in Africa; and a 362 letter to Bostra telling Christian citizens they could keep good standing only if they sacrificed to the traditional deities (paraphrase). For the pagan church: a letter of late 361/early 362 made Theodorus high priest of the diocese of Asia with power to appoint and vet priests for justice and piety; and a 362 letter to Arsacius, high priest of Galatia, directing priests to copy Christian charity, care for the dead, and holy living, barring priests from taverns, theatres, and base professions, ordering hostels in every Galatian city, and allocating one-fifth of 30,000 modii of wheat and 60,000 pints of wine to charity — practices Julian claimed dated to Homer (paraphrase). The page also records institutional friction: Alexandrians lynched bishop George after he threatened the temple of the emperor's Genius, and Julian's 362 letter rebuked the city for acting without consulting him; a letter to Hecebolius threatened to withdraw clemency from Edessa over Arian riots; and the Senate at Antioch refused support when Julian would not divert Persian-campaign resources to earthquake and drought relief, prompting his satire Misopogon. The page attributes Julian's Persian motives, via Socrates Scholasticus, to imitation of Alexander, while suggesting the real reason may have been his uneasy relations with top officers and the need to bind the army to him."
  },
  {
   "url": "https://edinburghuniversitypress.com/book-julian-the-apostate.html",
   "title": "Julian the Apostate",
   "snippet": "<strong>This study of the last pagan Roman emperor provides remarkable insight into the man and his times</strong>The figure of Julian demands the attention of historians. As the last pagan Roman Emperor, he provides a focus for studying the religious ...",
   "text": "This is the Edinburgh University Press catalogue page for Shaun Tougher's *Julian the Apostate*, published in paperback in January 2007 in the series Debates and Documents in Ancient History (224 pages, 234 mm x 156 mm; ISBN paperback 9780748618873, ebook 9781474473286; listed price $44.95, marked as reprinting at the time of capture). The publisher's description states that the book treats Julian as the last pagan Roman emperor and a focus for studying fourth-century religious, social and political transformations, and emphasises that Julian elicited sharply divided contemporary opinion, polarised between pagan supporters and Christian opponents, a division the description says is matched in modern literature. The page frames the book's guiding questions as whether Julian was a prospective saviour of the empire or out of touch, an evangelist for Mithraism or a more traditional pagan, and a shrewd military man or a rash risk-taker whose luck ran out on the Persian expedition. Listed key features are that issues of historical significance are identified and discussed, key passages of text are gathered in one volume, and modern interpretations are presented and considered.\n\nThe table of contents is relevant to the institutions slot because it enumerates the primary documentary corpus the book assembles. Part I (Debates) comprises an introduction on the fascination of Julian, chapters on Family, Conversion, Gaul, Emperor: Style and Reform, Religion, and Persia, and a conclusion on the elusiveness of Julian. Part II (Documents) lists 43 items, of which the page shows items 1 through 43 partially (the final visible entry is 'Gregory of Nazianzus: A', cut off). The documents by Julian himself include: Panegyric on the Emperor Constantius; Panegyric on the Empress Eusebia; On the Deeds of the Emperor Constantius or On Kingship; Consolation to Himself on the Departure of the Excellent Salutius; letters to Priscus, Oribasius, the Athenians, Maximus the Philosopher, Themistius the Philosopher, the Philosopher Maximus, Bishop Aetius, the People of Alexandria, Ecdicus Prefect of Egypt, Evagrius, the Thracians, the High-priest Theodorus, Arsacius High-priest of Galatia, Atarbius, the Citizens of Bostra, and the Alexandrians; Against the Cynic Heraclius; To the Alexandrians, an Edict; To the Mother of the Gods; Rescript on Christian Teachers; The Caesars; To King Helios; Letter to a Priest; and Misopogon. Non-Julian documents include Inscriptions, the Theodosian Code, Sextus Aurelius Victor's De Caesaribus, Claudius Mamertinus's Speech of Thanks to Julian, eight works by Libanius (Letter to Julian; Address to Julian; To Julian on behalf of Aristophanes; Address to the Emperor Julian as Consul; The Embassy to Julian; Letter to Aristophanes; Funeral Oration for Julian; Upon Avenging Julian; and his Autobiography — nine items in fact), Salutius's Concerning the Gods and the Universe, and the truncated Gregory of Nazianzus entry.\n\nThe author biography on the page states that Shaun Tougher is Professor of Late Roman and Byzantine History at Cardiff University, with a BA from Queen's University Belfast and a PhD from the University of St Andrews; that he works on dynastic history (primarily the Constantinian and Macedonian dynasties) and on gender and society (notably eunuchs, imperial women, and masculinity); and that his publications include *The Reign of Leo VI (886-912)* (Leiden, 1997), *Julian the Apostate* (Edinburgh, 2007), *The Eunuch in Byzantine History and Society* (London and New York, 2008), and *The Roman Castrati: Eunuchs in the Roman Empire* (London and New York, 2021)."
  },
  {
   "url": "https://dokumen.pub/a-companion-to-julian-the-apostate-2019046065-2019046066-9789004414563-9789004416314.html",
   "title": "A Companion to Julian the Apostate 2019046065, 2019046066, 9789004414563, 9789004416314 - DOKUMEN.PUB",
   "snippet": "10: “Faites pour un moment abstraction des vérités révélées; cherchez dans toute la nature, et vous n’y trouverez pas de plus grand objet que les Antonins; Julian même, Julien (un suffrage ainsi arraché ne me rendra point complice de son apostasie), non, il n’y a point eu après lui de prince plus digne de gouverner les hommes”. On Montesquieu, Voltaire and Diderot see Spink, “Julian the Apostate in the Enlightenment” (→ i.16); Boch, Apostat ou philosophe? (→ i.16), pp. 503-680 and passim. 6 Diderot’s views on Julian can be found in no. XLIII of his Pensées philosophiques (1746) and in the article Éclectisme written for volume V of the famous Encyclopédie ou Dictionnaire raisonné des sciences, des arts et des métiers (1755).",
   "text": "110 111 112 113 114 115 116\n\nI cite the translation of Ephraem in Lieu, The Emperor Julian (→ i.3), pp. 125-126. Griffith, “Ephraem the Syrian’s Hymns ‘Against Julian’” (→ iii.4), pp. 250-251 and 258-260. Shepardson, “Paschal Politics: Deploying the Temple’s Destruction against FourthCentury Judaizers”, p. 244. Shepardson, “Paschal Politics: Deploying the Temple’s Destruction against FourthCentury Judaizers”, pp. 233-260.\n\nIndex (compiled by Sabina Walter and Bernhard Kremer) All dates are AD unless otherwise indicated. Achilles, Greek demigod (altar of) 64, 110, 213 Aedesius, Greek philosopher (Neoplatonist, † 355) 328 Aegeae 219 n. 19, 267 n. 1 Aelius Aristides, Greek sophist († 181) 38 Africa, province of 23, 24 n. 47, 106, 131 n. 24, 138 n. 55, 139 n. 59, 141, 147, 158, 196, 210, 215, 219 n. 18, 328 Agri Decumates 78 n. 33, 83, 87 Alamanni 66–87, 89 n. 60, 91, 94–5, 118 (Lentienses), 298 n. 22 Alexander the Great (356–323 BC) 7, 9, 249 n. 20, 268, 297 n. 18, 299, 306, 309, 370, 371 n. 36, 400 Alexander of Lycopolis, Greek philosopher (Platonist, 3rd c.) 212, 213 n. 9 Alexander Severus, Roman emperor 222–35 34, 138 n. 56 Alexandria (Egypt) 7, 132 n. 29, 156–8, 175 n. 6 + 9, 214 n. 11, 220–1, 336, 346 n. 46, 348, 383, 412 Alypius of Antioch, comes in charge of rebuilding the temple in Jerusalem 157, 271 Ambrose of Milan, bishop and Latin theologian († 397) 188 n. 44, 220, 280, 361, 367 Amelius, Greek philosopher (Neoplatonist, 3rd c.) 258 Amida 320, 365, 390 Ammianus Marcellinus, Latin historian († c. 395) 270–1, 294–312, 314–22 Amorgus 34 Anatha 300-4, 316, 322 Anatolius of Berytus, praetorian prefect of Illyricum 357–60 328 Ancyra 180 n. 21, 220, 367, 378, 384–5, 388, 390 Antioch (Syria) 7, 9, 11, 55, 125, 131 n. 24, 133–5, 138 n. 55, 143–4, 150, 156, 160 n. 127,\n\n162, 166–7, 174–7, 180, 219, 222–4, 245, 267, 269–71, 285–6, 297, 309, 335–41, 345–6, 349, 356, 366–7, 377–8, 383–6, 388, 390–1 Antisthenes, Greek philosopher (Cynic, † 365 BC) 48 Ant