   "title": "Julian the Apostate 9781474473286 - DOKUMEN.PUB",
   "snippet": "<strong>This study of the last pagan Roman emperor provides remarkable insight into the man and his times</strong>The figure of Julian...",
   "text": "This page is a partial dump of a modern sourcebook on Julian the Apostate (the Edinburgh/''Julian the Apostate: 361-363'' volume), consisting of its table of contents, two body-text passages, a bibliography, and the index. The table of contents shows the volume's evidentiary base: Julian's own letters (to Bishop Aetius, to the People of Alexandria, to Ecdicius Prefect of Egypt, to the High-priest Theodorus, to Arsacius High-priest of Galatia, to Atarbius, to the citizens of Bostra, to the Thracians, to Evagrius), his Rescript on Christian Teachers, the Misopogon, The Caesars, To King Helios, To the Mother of the Gods, plus inscriptions, the Theodosian Code, Ammianus Marcellinus, Libanius (several speeches and the Funeral Oration), Salutius, Gregory of Nazianzus's two orations Against Julian, Ephrem the Syrian's Hymns against Julian, Eutropius, Festus, Jerome, John Chrysostom, and Eunapius.\n\nThe first body passage summarizes Julian's Letter to the Athenians (not quoting it directly): the page reports that Julian there depicts himself as a super-soldier and economic reformer, describing the poor condition of Gaul on his arrival, with German sackings of towns, his recovery of Cologne, and the battle of Strasbourg in 357, which he calls a famous victory. Julian claims Gaul was transformed — barbarians driven out, towns recovered, Roman ships again navigating the Rhine — and records going on the offensive against the Salii and Chamavi in 358. He presents his economic successes as bound up with the military ones, and mentions abandoning the policy of paying barbarians for passage on the Rhine, a policy devised by Florentius, the Praetorian Prefect, whose greed Julian says he opposed. The page says this self-portrait is largely supported by other sources, above all Ammianus, who was in Gaul from 355–7 under the general Ursicinus and whose account is recognized as the major testimony: Ammianus describes the recovery of Brumath (16.2), the Strasbourg battle as an extended set piece (16.12), and the 357–8 campaigns against Alamanni and Franks including the Salii and Chamavi (17.1–2, 17.8, 17.10), some of it across the Rhine, and fleshes out Julian's thin account of his financial measures (citing Matthews 1989). The page hedges that whether Julian's Letter included the detail that the army hailed him Augustus after Strasbourg is unknowable, and notes Ammianus appears to follow Julian's own lost account of the battle.\n\nThe second passage concerns the influence of Alexander the Great and Trajan on Julian's Persian ambitions. The page reports that the church historian Socrates alleges Julian believed he possessed the soul of Alexander (3.21.6–7), but hedges that this may be Christian mockery, akin to Gregory of Nazianzus's story that Julian wished to throw himself into the Tigris so his body would not be found, a tale previously told of Alexander. Julian's The Caesars, written at Antioch in 362, has the gods include Alexander among six finalists in a competition for best ruler, with Trajan also a finalist who at the end flocks to Alexander as his own inspiration. Among moderns, Athanassiadi and Baynes argue strongly for Alexander's importance to Julian; Smith is unconvinced; Lane Fox argues Julian viewed Alexander dispassionately and that such interest was normal for an emperor concerned with Persia, citing the Itinerary of Alexander written for Constantius II.\n\nThe index, though fragmentary, shows the volume's institutional coverage with page references: the agentes in rebus; the trials at Chalcedon (pp. 45–6); city councillors and councils, including the council of Antioch; the senate of Constantinople and the senate of Rome; crown gold; the cursus publicus; taxation; the teaching edict (Julian's rescript on Christian teachers, discussed across numerous pages); coinage including the 'bull coinage'; the university at Athens; the Eleusinian mysteries and the hierophant of Demeter's cult; the attempted rebuilding of the temple of Solomon in Jerusalem; pagan priests including Arsacius, high-priest of Galatia, and the priest of Apollo at Daphne; and Florentius, Praetorian Prefect."
  },
  {
   "url": "https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2007/2007.09.36",
   "title": "Julian the Apostate. Debates and Documents in Ancient History series – Bryn Mawr Classical Review",
   "snippet": "3). Chapters 4 and 5, while still in line with the general chronology, are more thematic in character: they discuss the ‘imperial style’ of Julian (Ch. 4) and his religion (Ch. 5). Accompanying the volume’s historical overview of Julian are a timeline, a family tree of the emperor, and three maps of the Empire; a guided ‘further reading’ section, a section with ‘essay questions and exercise topics’, and a list of Internet resources.",
   "text": "This page is Sergio Knipe's 2007 Bryn Mawr Classical Review notice of Shaun Tougher's introductory volume \"Julian the Apostate\" (Edinburgh University Press, 2007, xiii + 201 pp.), and it reports the book's contents rather than recording Julian directly. Knipe reports that Tougher treats Julian's economic policy in Gaul and his reform of court finance as revolutionary, evidence of what Tougher calls a \"practical streak\" (p. 46), and describes Julian in Gaul as \"a super soldier and an economic whiz kid\" (p. 33). On religious institutions, Knipe criticises Tougher for presenting Julian's letters to his priests — implicitly following R. Asmus (1896) — as a deliberate effort to educate the clergy of a newly established \"pagan Church\" (p. 58), noting that Mario Mazza has argued the letters' purpose, and hence the nature of Julian's religious reform, is far less obvious. Knipe also faults Tougher for ignoring Peter van Nuffelen's suggestion that the so-called Letter to Arsacius may be a fifth-century fabrication, and for never critically engaging with the problem of Julian's organisation of the pagan clergy. Knipe notes that Tougher raises questions about the nature of Julian's accession — Tougher describes the usurpation as possibly the product of a \"pagan conspiracy\" — without citing Jean Bouffartigue's 1998 article on Julian's anti-Christian action. The review lists the primary sources Tougher anthologises: selections from Julian himself, Libanius, Eunapius, Ammianus, Zosimus, Eutropius, Festus, Mamertinus' panegyric of 362, excerpts from the Theodosian Code, four inscriptions, Christian authors, three coin types and the Louvre statue; Knipe notes as omissions Julian's oration Against the Uneducated Cynics and the Latin inscription from Ma'ayan Barukh praising Julian as restaurator templorum (\"restorer of the temples\"), and that only Orosius is freshly translated by Tougher. Knipe's overall judgment is that the book raises questions rather than answering them and offers little beyond standard introductions."
  }
