   "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Julian_(emperor)",
   "title": "Julian (emperor) - Wikipedia",
   "snippet": "↑ David Sear, Roman Coins and Their Values, <strong>Volume</strong> <strong>5</strong> (<strong>London</strong>: <strong>Spink</strong>, <strong>2014</strong>), p.",
   "text": "The Wikipedia reference article on Julian (Flavius Claudius Julianus, born Constantinople 331, died 26 June 363 near Samarra; Caesar in the West 355–360, emperor 361–363) provides mostly third-person summary of the fourth-century world he moved through. It reports that after Constantine's death in 337, Julian's cousin Constantius II appears to have led a massacre of most of Julian's close relatives — the article hedges with 'appears' and 'allegedly' — leaving Julian and his half-brother Gallus among the few surviving imperial males; the two boys were excluded from public life, strictly guarded, and given a Christian education, 'likely saved by their youth.' The article adds that if Julian's later writings are to be believed, Constantius was afterward tormented by guilt over the massacre.\n\nJulian's own retrospective framing is quoted: looking back in 362, he wrote that he had spent twenty years in the way of Christianity and twelve in the true way, that of Helios, putting his conversion around age twenty. The article recounts, via the historian Eunapius, ordinary features of philosophical education in Asia Minor in 351: Julian studied under Aedesius, then Eusebius of Myndus, who described watching the theurgist Maximus of Ephesus make a statue of Hecate smile and its torches ignite. Eusebius's warning is given in quotation: Julian 'must not marvel at any of these things, even as I marvel not, but rather believe that the thing of the highest importance is that purification of the soul which is attained by reason,' alongside his talk of the 'impostures of witchcraft and magic that cheat the senses.' Julian's reply on leaving is also quoted: 'farewell, and devote yourself to your books. You have shown me the man I was in search of.'\n\nThe article records the ordinary mechanics of mid-century administration: in 354 Julian was held a year at Milan under suspicion of intrigue and was cleared partly through Empress Eusebia's intervention (the article notes a scholarly caveat, citing Shaun Tougher, that the kind Eusebia of Julian's panegyric may be a literary creation and that she may have been doing Constantius's bidding). In Gaul at the end of 357 Julian blocked a tax increase by the praetorian prefect Florentius and took personal charge of Belgica Secunda, clashing with Florentius over taxation and bureaucratic corruption while trying to rebuild devastated cities and win over the civil population. In February 360 Constantius's order transferring more than half of Julian's Gallic troops east — sent directly to the commanders, bypassing Julian — provoked a mutiny of the Petulantes, who did not want to leave Gaul; Zosimus is cited for officers distributing an anonymous tract, and Ammianus Marcellinus for the suggestion that Constantius acted from fear of Julian's popularity at Florentius's urging. Julian's own later justification (late November 361) is quoted: he set out 'because, having been declared a public enemy, I meant to frighten him merely, and that our quarrel should result in intercourse on more friendly terms.'\n\nOn ordinary religious and civic life, the article describes an empire where the old system of sacrifice and communal festival had already collapsed: civic leaders lacked funds and support for festivals because Constantine had seized the sacred temple funds to support the Church; Christians had denounced sacrifice and cut priests off from prestige and income; and politicians preferred religiously neutral ceremonies and mass entertainment rather than reviving pagan festivals. Christian charities were open to all, including pagans, which Julian resented as slipping beyond imperial control; his remark is quoted: 'These impious Galileans not only feed their own poor, but ours also; welcoming them into their agapae, they attract them, as children are attracted, with cakes.' The article dates his religious measures: an edict of 4 February 362 proclaiming all religions equal before the law — 'seen as an act of favor toward the Jews, in order to upset the Christians' — and the School Edict requiring imperial approval of public teachers, whose salaries the state paid or supplemented, with the edict quoted: 'If they want to learn literature, they have Luke and Mark: Let them go back to their churches and expound on them.' At Delphi, told that Bishop Babylas's bones were suppressing the oracle, Julian ordered them removed, provoking a massive Christian procession; when the temple then burned, he suspected Christians and closed Antioch's chief church before investigations showed the fire was accidental.\n\nFor the Persian campaign the article gives checkable specifics with their hedges: Julian left Antioch on 5 March 363 despite adverse omens, with about 65,000–83,000 or 80,000–90,000 men (Gibbon's traditional figure of 95,000 noted), a fleet of over 1,000 ships built at Samosata plus 50 pontoon ships, a diversionary force of 30,000 under Procopius and Sebastianus, an apparent aim of replacing Shapur II with his brother Hormisdas, the burning of the fleet after the generals' council declined to besiege Ctesiphon, and the council of 16 June 363 deciding on retreat north to Corduene. The famous dying words 'Vicisti, Galilaee' ('You have won, Galilean') are flagged by the article itself as apocryphal, traceable to Theodoret in the fifth century — a distinction between later legend and record that is retained here as such.\n\nAlso kept as quoted testimony of ordinary belief: Julian's Timaeus-based account that 'when Zeus was setting all things in order there fell from him drops of sacred blood, and from them, as they say, arose the race of men,' his argument against a single first couple — 'how very different in their bodies are the Germans and Scythians from the Libyans and Ethiopians' — and Socrates Scholasticus's claim that Julian believed himself Alexander the Great reborn 'in accordance with the teachings of Pythagoras and Plato.' The failure to rebuild the Jerusalem Temple is reported with its hedges: possibly the Galilee earthquake of 363; Gregory Nazianzen's contemporary account of wind, earth-heaving, and flame is given as what Gregory says 'all people nowadays report and believe,' with Gibbon's later skepticism (sabotage or accident) noted."
