  {
   "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Julian_(emperor)",
   "title": "Julian (emperor) - Wikipedia",
   "snippet": "<strong>Julian</strong> (Latin: Flavius Claudius ... Roman <strong>emperor</strong> from 361 to 363, as well as a notable philosopher and author in Greek. His rejection of Christianity, public conversion to paganism, and promotion of Neoplatonic Hellenism caused him to be remembered as <strong>Julian</strong> <strong>the</strong> <strong>Apostate</strong> in the Christian ...",
   "text": "Julian (Flavius Claudius Julianus; born Constantinople 331, died 26 June 363 near Samarra) was Caesar in the West from 355 and emperor from 3 November 361 until his death, succeeding Constantius II and succeeded by Jovian. In the turmoil after Constantine's death in 337, his cousin Constantius II appears to have led a massacre of most of Julian's close male relatives; Julian and his half-brother Gallus were spared, the page suggests, likely because of their youth, and were strictly guarded and given a Christian education. If Julian's later writings are to be believed, Constantius was afterward tormented by guilt over the massacre. Looking back in 362, Julian wrote that he had spent twenty years in the way of Christianity and twelve in the true way, that of Helios; his conversion to paganism came around age twenty. He began studying Neoplatonism in Asia Minor in 351 under Aedesius and then Eusebius of Myndus. Eusebius described meeting Maximus of Ephesus, who in the temple of Hecate chanted a hymn and made the goddess's statue smile and her torches ignite, but warned Julian, in the page's quoted words, that he 'must not marvel at any of these things, even as I marvel not,' since the highest thing was purification of the soul by reason, and warned against the 'impostures of witchcraft and magic that cheat the senses.' Julian sought out Maximus anyway; according to Eunapius, he told Eusebius on leaving, 'farewell, and devote yourself to your books. You have shown me the man I was in search of.' In 354, after Gallus was executed for a rule of terror in the East, Julian was held a year at Milan under suspicion of intrigue, was cleared partly through Empress Eusebia's intervention (one cited scholar, Tougher, argues the kind Eusebia of Julian's panegyric is a literary creation and she was doing Constantius's bidding), and was allowed to study in Athens, where he knew Gregory of Nazianzus and Basil and was initiated into the Eleusinian Mysteries. At the end of 357, as Caesar in Gaul, he blocked a tax increase by the prefect Florentius and took personal charge of Belgica Secunda, clashing repeatedly with Florentius over taxes and bureaucratic corruption. In February 360 Constantius ordered more than half of Julian's Gallic troops east, by-passing Julian; the Petulantes mutinied and proclaimed Julian emperor. Zosimus reports officers distributed an anonymous tract against Constantius; Ammianus Marcellinus even suggested Constantius sent the order out of fear of Julian's popularity, urged by Florentius. In spring 361 Julian captured the Alamannic king Vadomarius, claiming he had been in league with Constantius, and advanced toward civil war; Julian stated in late November that he marched 'because, having been declared a public enemy, I meant to frighten him [Constantius] merely.' As emperor he idealized Hadrian and Marcus Aurelius, described the ideal ruler in his first panegyric as primus inter pares, and frequently sat and debated in the Senate as one member among others. He ordered the bones of bishop Babylas removed from near Apollo's temple at Daphne, provoking a massive Christian procession; when the temple then burned, he suspected Christians, shut Antioch's chief church, and ordered stricter investigations, which found the fire accidental. On 5 March 363, despite adverse omens, he left Antioch with about 65,000–90,000 men (Gibbon's accepted figure is 95,000), aiming, the page says, at 'regime change'—replacing Shapur II with his brother Hormisdas. The army won a tactical victory before Ctesiphon but, on his generals' advice, did not besiege it; Julian burned his fleet and marched inland, a decision the page calls hasty, and a council of war on 16 June 363 resolved to retreat via Corduene. He died of a wound on 26 June 363, aged 31–32. The report that his dying words were 'You have won, Galilean' (νενίκηκάς με, Γαλιλαῖε) is considered apocryphal, appearing in Theodoret in the 5th century; Swinburne's 1866 poem and Krasiński's 1833 play reuse it. On what was ordinary then: Julian accepted the creation account of Plato's Timaeus, writing that when Zeus set all things in order drops of sacred blood fell from him from which arose the race of men, and arguing against descent from a single pair by noting 'how very different in their bodies are the Germans and Scythians from the Libyans and Ethiopians.' Socrates Scholasticus claims Julian believed himself Alexander the Great in another body, per Pythagorean and Platonic transmigration. His religious policy aimed not to destroy Christianity but to drive it from the governing classes. His edict of 4 February 362 proclaimed all religions equal before the law; the page reports it was seen as favoring the Jews to upset Christians. His School Edict required imperial approval of public teachers, whose salaries the state paid or supplemented; Ammianus explains it as barring Christians from teaching the pagan classics, quoting the edict: 'If they want to learn literature, they have Luke and Mark: Let them go back to their churches and expound on them.' By Julian's accession the old order was already gone: massive public sacrifices had ended, communal festivals now divided Christian from pagan, civic leaders lacked funds for festivals because Constantine had seized sacred temple funds for the Church, and politicians preferred religiously neutral ceremonies and mass entertainment. Christian charities were open to all including pagans, so Julian envisioned a pagan philanthropic system, writing: 'These impious Galileans not only feed their own poor, but ours also; welcoming them into their agapae, they attract them, as children are attracted, with cakes.' His attempt to rebuild the Jerusalem Temple failed; Gregory Nazianzen describes the builders driven against one another as by a blast of wind and heaving earth, with flame issuing from a church, and says this is 'what all people nowadays report and believe.' Gibbon judged this unreliable, positing sabotage or accident, while divine intervention is a common view among Christian historians. The failure may be ascribed to the Galilee earthquake of 363."
