  {
   "url": "https://earlychristianhistory.net/julian.html",
   "title": "Early Christian History: Extras — Emperor Julian “the Apostate”",
   "snippet": "By the end of the century, most pagan practices would be outlawed, leaving Christianity the only religion standing — aside from Mithras, which still lurked among the Roman legions and, possibly, the aristocracy. It almost goes without saying that Emperor Julian “the Apostate” is among the worst villains of history, as least in the eyes of Christianity.",
   "text": "The page, from a site called Early Christian History, is a sympathetic modern reappraisal of the Emperor Julian; its author is openly hostile to the Christian tradition about him, dismissing Christian writers' complaints in scornful terms, and that slant should be kept in mind throughout. It reports that Julian was born in 331 CE to a younger half-brother of Constantine the Great; after Constantine died in 337, his three sons succeeded amid a bloodbath that killed much of the family, including Julian's father (his mother had died a few years earlier), leaving Julian, in the page's phrase, more or less an orphaned political prisoner in the care of various functionaries, some of them Christian clergy, and later shipped to Cappadocia. He received a typical eastern Roman education, and from age 18 moved among Constantinople, Nicomedia — where he served as a church lector — and Athens. The page stresses that he received as deep an education in Christianity as any layman of the era could have, tended by well-respected bishops. Constantius II, sole surviving son of Constantine, kept Julian under watch in Milan while Julian's half-brother Gallus was made Caesar; after Gallus became intolerable and was murdered, Constantius named Julian Caesar in 355 and sent him to Gaul, which the page reads as an attempt to sideline him. Despite no military training, Julian recaptured Cologne and other territories while outnumbered; the page attributes his troops' devotion partly to the fact that he lived, walked, talked, and ate among them. In early 360 Constantius ordered more than half of Julian's best men east against Persia; the troops refused, the page says, knowing Julian was being set up for assassination, and proclaimed him Augustus. Civil war threatened almost a year later but was pre-empted by Constantius's death, and Julian entered Constantinople in late 361 as sole emperor.\n\nAround his accession Julian revealed he was a pagan: initiated into the Eleusinian Mysteries, a student of Neoplatonic theurgy, a reverer of Helios and of Cybele (Mater Magna). He wrote a Greek treatise titled κατα γαλιλαιων (kata galilaion), in Latin Contra Galilaeos, Against the Galileans — the page notes Julian called Christians \"Galileans\" after Jesus of Galilee. The page argues his critique was well-informed and largely logical rather than ignorant or demonic, as Christian legend held. Because he understood Christianity's reverence for martyrdom, the page says, he avoided violent persecution: he never overturned the Edict of Milan, outlawed the faith, or jailed anyone for following it, and nearly all his measures targeted clergy and hierarchs rather than lay Christians. His first orders forbade Christians to teach rhetoric, philosophy, geometry, history, or classical literature, on the stated grounds that they did not believe the ancient authors they taught; the page notes this appeared mild to Julian since Christians could still teach their own theology, but it cost many clergy their income from teaching the general public. He permitted Arian practice and undid sanctions on Arian clergy — only slightly further, the page says, than Constantine's sons and the aging Constantine himself had gone — in hopes of stirring trouble within the Church. He issued rules protecting Jews, which the page reads as strategy against Christians rather than regard for Judaism, since his critiques of Christianity applied equally to Judaism; he also considered and began planning to rebuild the Temple in Jerusalem.\n\nFor paganism, Julian tried to build what the page calls a Church of Theurgy: a new appointed priesthood meant eventually to replace Christianity, with rules of conduct and appearance; recruitment was slow because his philosophically inflected standards were hard to meet. He took up the office of pontifex maximus and performed its rites to a degree not seen since Diocletian, and reinstituted animal sacrifice. He diverted funds previously granted to Christian institutions — episcopal treasuries for widows and orphans, money for catechetical instructors, funds feeding hermits and stylites — to his own projects, which the page says enraged the hierarchy. He cultivated the image of a second Marcus Aurelius, wearing a beard as emperors had not for some time; Christians resented the comparison because they remembered Marcus Aurelius as a persecutor, though the page hedges that the degree to which Marcus Aurelius actually persecuted Christians beyond other emperors is not at all clear — persecution rose during his reign, but so did Christianity's numbers, and he instituted nothing like Diocletian's program.\n\nJulian moved to Antioch, a majority-Christian city, to prepare the Persian campaign. A dispute erupted over a saint's remains interred on a pagan temple's grounds, which Julian ordered exhumed as defiling the temple; the temple then burned, presumably, the page says, at Christian hands, and Julian shut the city's cathedral. The page dates the solidifying of his persecutor reputation to this point. His Persian campaign began in early 363; in June he was wounded in battle and died the next day or the day after. Christians read the death as divine judgment; the page also records the rumor that he was assassinated by one of his own men, while noting almost no one buys it, even among those who heard it within a couple of years. The army acclaimed Jovian, a Christian commander of the bodyguard, who conceded terms to Persia — considered shameful for the period, the page says — and was met at Edessa by Church hierarchs including Athanasius, who hoped for restoration as Patriarch of Alexandria; Jovian restored Christian favors and Athanasius's office, removed some Arian bishops Julian had reinstated, and then died under mysterious circumstances before reaching Constantinople, which the page notes grateful Christians never questioned as they had questioned Julian's death. Julian's Church of Theurgy withered within a few years, diverted funds were restored to the Church, and the Temple project ended; by century's end most pagan practice was outlawed, with Mithras possibly lingering among legions and aristocracy. The page also catalogues Christian writers' personal attacks — his head-tilting tic read as demonic possession, his talkativeness, his dismissal of Constantius's officials, his Athens years — and its own counter-assessment: that Julian was vain, thin-skinned to flattery, and off-putting, but never ordered massacres as Constantius II had, and was, in the author's conclusion, more virtuous than several Christian emperors of the era."
