   "url": "https://christiancourier.com/articles/the-strange-case-of-julian-the-apostate",
   "title": "The Strange Case of Julian \"The Apostate\" | Christian Courier",
   "snippet": "The emperor issued a prohibition against Christian schools. One historian notes: “The Christian schools were broken up, and the children of Christians denied all education save in the school of the idolaters” (Abbott n.d., 335). Julian knew that the church’s future is with its youth.",
   "text": "Wayne Jackson, writing for Christian Courier (a Church of Christ apologetics site), gives a polemical account of Julian 'the Apostate' (reigned A.D. 361-363), framing him as a failed enemy of Christianity; the article is summarising standard church histories, not recording primary testimony. Jackson recounts that after Constantine's Edict of Toleration (A.D. 313) and death in A.D. 337, his three sons, aged twenty-one, twenty, and seventeen, divided the empire and murdered uncles and cousins as rivals; the young Gallus and Julian, then five or six, were spared and sent under house imprisonment in a remote part of Cappadocia with austere discipline and some education. Gallus later ruled in the east and freed Julian, who studied in Constantinople under pagan philosophers; Jackson argues Julian's hatred of Christianity grew from abuse by professing Christians combined with humanistic teaching, and that he concealed it for a decade while outwardly performing church functions. Elevated emperor in A.D. 361 at barely over thirty, he declared himself publicly against Christianity that year. Jackson describes his extreme pagan asceticism: he abandoned luxury, slept on the ground, went unwashed and disheveled, and — per Jackson's paraphrase of one of Julian's own letters — boasted of his long nails, shaggy head, and dirty hands. Jackson lists Julian's methods, each paired with a modern sermon application: encouraging strife by restoring exiled antagonistic bishops; prohibiting Christian schools, with Jackson quoting the historian Abbott that 'The Christian schools were broken up, and the children of Christians denied all education save in the school of the idolaters' (Abbott n.d., 335); removing Christians from public positions; withdrawing immunities Constantine had granted preachers; and tolerating violence — Jackson quotes John Hurst (1897, 421) that Julian did not 'punish his heathen subjects for acts of violence committed against the Christians' yet took pains 'to punish a Christian for the slightest offense.' Jackson also describes Julian's subsidised project to rebuild the Jerusalem temple — official praise of the Jews, eased taxes, treasury funds, a promise to officiate at consecration — which Jackson says failed amid fires and storms, noting the disagreement among historians: Gibbon treated these as natural disasters, others as supernatural judgment, and others, citing Schaff (1981, 56-57), as providential impediments. Finally Jackson reports that Julian wrote a three-volume Adversus Christianos, now lost and known only through Christian replies such as Cyril of Alexandria's Contra Julianum written some sixty years after Julian's death; Jackson, following Nathaniel Lardner's eighteenth-century Credibility of the Gospel History, says Julian conceded Christ's historicity and referred to healings, exorcisms, the calming of the storm, walking on water, and the early dates of the Gospels, Acts, and some Pauline writings while trying to explain them away, and quotes Lardner via McClintock (1969, 1090): 'He aimed to overthrow the Christian religion, but has confirmed it: his arguments against it are perfectly harmless, and insufficient to unsettle the weakest Christian.' Julian died in battle against the Persians in A.D. 363, aged thirty-two, after reigning twenty months."
