   "url": "https://edinburghuniversitypress.com/book-julian-the-apostate.html",
   "title": "Julian the Apostate",
   "snippet": "<strong>This study of the last pagan Roman emperor provides remarkable insight into the man and his times</strong>The figure of Julian demands the attention of historians. As the last pagan Roman Emperor, he provides a focus for studying the religious ...",
   "text": "This Edinburgh University Press page describes Shaun Tougher's 'Julian the Apostate' (published January 2007, paperback, 224 pages, ISBN 9780748618873, in the Debates and Documents in Ancient History series). The publisher's description characterises Julian as the last pagan Roman emperor and presents the fourth-century empire as a period of religious transformation, alongside social and political change addressed by Julian's secular policies. The description reports that Julian elicited sharply divided opinion from contemporaries, polarised largely between pagan supporters and Christian opponents, and that modern literature is similarly divided — over whether he was a prospective saviour of the empire or out of touch, an evangelist for Mithraism or a more traditional pagan, a shrewd military man or a rash risk-taker whose luck ran out on his Persian expedition. The book's contents list the primary documents it collects, which serve as a checkable inventory of fourth-century source material: Julian's own writings (panegyrics on Constantius and the Empress Eusebia; letters to Priscus, Oribasius, the Athenians, Maximus, Themistius, Bishop Aetius, the people of Alexandria, the prefect Ecdicus, Evagrius, the Thracians, the high-priest Theodorus, Arsacius of Galatia, Atarbius, the citizens of Bostra; the Rescript on Christian Teachers; Against the Cynic Heraclius; To the Mother of the Gods; To King Helios; The Caesars; Misopogon), plus inscriptions, the Theodosian Code, Sextus Aurelius Victor's De Caesaribus, Claudius Mamertinus' speech of thanks to Julian, multiple works of Libanius including a funeral oration, Salutius' Concerning the Gods and the Universe, and a work of Gregory of Nazianzus (title truncated on the page). The author biography states that Shaun Tougher is Professor of Late Roman and Byzantine History at Cardiff University, with a BA from Queen's University Belfast and PhD from St Andrews, working on Constantinian and Macedonian dynastic history and on gender and society including eunuchs. The page itself contains no direct testimony about Julian; it is modern marketing copy summarising a study."
  },
