   "snippet": "<strong>This study of the last pagan Roman emperor provides remarkable insight into the man and his times</strong>The figure of Julian...",
   "text": "This page is a bookseller's dump of a scholarly sourcebook on Julian (emperor 361–363); most of it is table of contents, bibliography and index, with two substantive prose passages, which I paraphrase here (confidence: high that I reflect the page's content; the page itself summarises rather than transcribes its sources, and I quote nothing verbatim because the page offers no verbatim utterances).\n\nThe page reports that Julian's Letter to the Athenians presents his Gallic record: on his arrival Gaul was in poor condition, Germanic groups having sacked towns and settled near some of them; he celebrates his recovery of Cologne and the battle of Strasbourg in 357, which he calls a famous victory, claims the barbarians were driven out and Roman ships could again navigate the Rhine, and records going on the offensive against the Salii and Chamavi in 358. He ties his economic success to his military one, noting especially that he abandoned the policy of paying the barbarians for passage on the Rhine — a policy the page says was devised by Florentius, the Praetorian Prefect, against whose greed Julian generally opposed himself. The page reports that Ammianus Marcellinus, who was in Gaul from 355–357 under the general Ursicinus, is recognised as the major testimony corroborating this, describing the recovery of Brumath, assaults on Alamanni sheltering on Rhine islands, an extended set-piece on Strasbourg, and later campaigning against Alamanni and Franks across the Rhine; the page notes Ammianus may have followed Julian's own lost account of the battle, and that whether Julian mentioned being hailed Augustus by the army after Strasbourg is unknowable. Ammianus is said to flesh out Julian's thin account of the financial measures, with Florentius again central.\n\nThe second passage concerns Julian's models. The page reports that scholars have pointed to Alexander the Great and Trajan as idols explaining his Persian ambitions: the church historian Socrates alleges Julian believed he possessed Alexander's soul, though the page flags this may be Christian mockery, akin to Gregory of Nazianzus' story that Julian wished to throw himself into the Tigris so his body would vanish and he would be thought divine — a story previously told of Alexander. Julian's own The Caesars, written at Antioch in 362, has the gods include both Alexander and Trajan among the final contestants for best ruler, with Trajan flocking to Alexander as his own inspiration. Among moderns, the page names Athanassiadi and Baynes as advocates of Alexander's importance, Smith as unconvinced, and Lane Fox as arguing Julian viewed Alexander dispassionately and that an interest in Alexander was not unusual for a Roman emperor concerned with Persia — witness an Itinerary of Alexander written for Constantius II. That last observation is the page's clearest note on what was ordinary for the period: Persian war, emulation of Alexander, and buying off frontier tribes were routine features of fourth-century Roman rule, not eccentricities."
