   "url": "https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2007/2007.09.36",
   "title": "Julian the Apostate. Debates and Documents in Ancient History series – Bryn Mawr Classical Review",
   "snippet": "3). Chapters 4 and 5, while still in line with the general chronology, are more thematic in character: they discuss the ‘imperial style’ of Julian (Ch. 4) and his religion (Ch. 5). Accompanying the volume’s historical overview of Julian are a timeline, a family tree of the emperor, and three maps of the Empire; a guided ‘further reading’ section, a section with ‘essay questions and exercise topics’, and a list of Internet resources.",
   "text": "This page is a 2007 Bryn Mawr Classical Review notice (BMCR 2007.09.36) by Sergio Knipe of the University of Cambridge, reviewing Shaun Tougher's Julian the Apostate (Edinburgh University Press, 2007, xiii + 201 pages, ISBN 0748618864), an introductory volume in the Debates and Documents in Ancient History series. Knipe opens by quoting Walter E. Kaegi's 1993 lament that the preceding decade had produced few works on Julian, and observes that by 2007 the opposite held: the previous ten years had seen a flourishing of academic studies, popular biographies and novels on the emperor, among which Knipe names the biographies of Bringmann (2004) and Rosen (2006) and the novels of Ford (2002) and Spector (2006).\n\nKnipe reports that Tougher describes his own book as an attempt to indicate the divergent views of Julian that exist and to supply the key evidence behind those interpretations (this is a paraphrase of wording the review quotes from page ix). The book's Part I offers six chapters moving chronologically from Julian's childhood through his conversion, the Gallic campaigns, his imperial style and religion, to the Persian expedition; Part II assembles primary texts in translation. Knipe reports that Tougher repeatedly encourages readers to make up their own minds, and acknowledges giving only what the review quotes as a partial account. Tougher emphasises the revolutionary character of Julian's economic policy in Gaul and court-finance reform, and describes Julian's usurpation as possibly the product of a pagan conspiracy — a point Knipe criticises for ignoring Bouffartigue's 1998 article on the alleged pagan party.\n\nPart II's documentary selection, as Knipe catalogues it, spans Julian himself, Libanius, Eunapius, Ammianus, Zosimus, Eutropius, Festus, the 362 panegyric of Mamertinus, excerpts from the Theodosian Code, four inscriptions, Christian authors, three coin types and the Louvre statue of Julian. Knipe notes as omissions Julian's oration Against the Uneducated Cynics and the Ma'ayan Barukh inscription praising Julian as restorer of temples, and regrets that Tougher mostly reprints standard English translations — those of Socrates and Philostorgius dating to 1890 and 1855 — with only Orosius freshly translated. Knipe also reports that Tougher, implicitly following Asmus (1896), treats Julian's letters to his priests as an effort to educate the clergy of a newly established pagan church, whereas Mazza has argued the letters' purpose is far less obvious, and van Nuffeln has suggested the so-called Letter to Arsacius may be a fifth-century fabrication. Knipe's overall verdict is that the book raises questions rather than answering them, serves inexperienced readers, but shows a decidedly anglophone bias in its bibliography and offers little beyond standard introductions."
  }
