   "text": "Julian (Flavius Claudius Julianus; 331 – 26 June 363) was Caesar of the West from 355 and Roman emperor from 361 to 363. Born in Constantinople to Julius Constantius and Basilina, he was a nephew of Constantine. After Constantine's death in 337, his cousin Constantius II appears to have led a massacre of most of Julian's close relatives — the page hedges with 'appears' and 'allegedly' — leaving Julian and his half-brother Gallus among the few surviving male relatives, likely saved by their youth. The two were excluded from public life, strictly guarded, and given a Christian education. The page adds the hedge that if Julian's later writings are to be believed, Constantius was later tormented by guilt over the massacre.\n\nJulian's conversion from Christianity to paganism came at about age 20. Looking back in 362, Julian wrote that he had spent twenty years in the way of Christianity and twelve in the true way, that of Helios. He began studying Neoplatonism in Asia Minor in 351 under Aedesius and then Aedesius' student Eusebius of Myndus, who told him of Maximus of Ephesus and his theurgy — including a demonstration in which a statue of Hecate smiled and her torches ignited. Eusebius warned against such impostures, but Julian sought out Maximus anyway; according to the historian Eunapius, Julian told Eusebius on leaving: 'farewell, and devote yourself to your books. You have shown me the man I was in search of.'\n\nHis path to power ran through dynastic catastrophe. Constantine II died attacking Constans in 340; Constans fell to the usurper Magnentius in 350; Constantius II made Gallus caesar of the East in 351, then executed him in 354 after a rule of terror. Julian was summoned to Milan in 354 and held for a year under suspicion of treasonable intrigue; he was cleared partly because Empress Eusebia intervened — though the page's footnote cites Shaun Tougher arguing that the kind Eusebia of Julian's panegyric is a literary creation doing Constantius' bidding. Julian was permitted to study in Athens, where he knew Gregory of Nazianzus and Basil the Great, and was initiated into the Eleusinian Mysteries, which he later tried to restore.\n\nAs Caesar in Gaul, at the end of 357 he used the prestige of his victory over the Alamanni to block a tax increase by the praetorian prefect Florentius and personally took charge of Belgica Secunda — properly the prefect's role — his first experience of civil administration. He clashed repeatedly with Florentius over taxes and corruption. In February 360, after Shapur II took Amida, Constantius ordered more than half of Julian's Gallic troops east, bypassing Julian; the order provoked an insurrection by the Petulantes. Zosimus reports that army officers distributed an anonymous tract complaining against Constantius and fearing for Julian's fate; Florentius was notably absent, and Ammianus Marcellinus even suggested Constantius sent the order out of fear of Julian's popularity, at Florentius' urging. The troops acclaimed Julian emperor in Paris in February 360.\n\nIn spring 361 Julian captured the Alamannic king Vadomarius, claiming he had been in league with Constantius, then advanced down the Danube toward civil war. Julian stated in late November that he set out 'because, having been declared a public enemy, I meant to frighten him [Constantius] merely, and that our quarrel should result in intercourse on more friendly terms.' Constantius died that November, leaving Julian sole emperor.\n\nAs emperor he rejected his predecessors' style, blaming Constantine for the state of the administration, idealizing Hadrian and Marcus Aurelius, and describing the ideal ruler in his first panegyric as primus inter pares, 'first among equals,' operating under the same laws as his subjects; in Constantinople he frequently sat in the Senate debating as one member among others.\n\nHis religious reformation shows what an emperor of his position could attempt. On 4 February 362 he promulgated an edict guaranteeing freedom of religion, declaring all religions equal before the law — seen, the page reports, as favor toward the Jews intended to upset Christians. His School Edict required imperial approval of all public teachers; Ammianus Marcellinus explains it as barring Christian teachers from pagan texts, quoting the edict: 'If they want to learn literature, they have Luke and Mark: Let them go back to their churches and expound on them.' The page notes the edict dealt a severe financial blow to Christian teachers. His aim, the page reports, was not to destroy Christianity but to drive it out of 'the governing classes of the empire.' At Antioch he tried to revive Apollo's oracle at Delphi's Castalian spring; told that the bones of bishop Babylas were suppressing the god, he ordered their removal — a public-relations mistake that produced a massive Christian procession — and when the temple then burned, he suspected Christians, ordered stricter investigations, and shut the chief Christian church before the fire proved accidental.\n\nThe structural limits on what he could do are recorded plainly. The temple funds that once financed pagan festivals had been seized by his uncle Constantine for the Church; civic leaders lacked funds and motivation, preferring religiously neutral entertainment. Christian charity being open to all put that part of life under Church control, so Julian envisioned a Roman philanthropic system, writing: 'These impious Galileans not only feed their own poor, but ours also; welcoming them into their agapae, they attract them, as children are attracted, with cakes.' His attempt to rebuild the Jerusalem Temple failed; the failure may be ascribed to the Galilee earthquake of 363, though Gregory Nazianzen describes the builders driven apart by wind and earth-heaving and a flame issuing from a church — which Gregory says is 'what all people nowadays report and believe' — while Gibbon considered this unreliable, positing sabotage or accident; divine intervention remains a common view among Christian historians.\n\nHis philosophy, from his Neoplatonist background, accepted Plato's Timaeus on human origins: 'when Zeus was setting all things in order there fell from him drops of sacred blood, and from them, as they say, arose the race of men,' and he argued against a single-pair origin, noting 'how very different in their bodies are the Germans and Scythians from the Libyans and Ethiopians.' Socrates Scholasticus claims Julian believed himself Alexander the Great 'in another body' by transmigration of souls.\n\nHis final campaign shows both reach and limit. On 5 March 363, despite omens against it, he left Antioch with about 65,000–83,000 or 80,000–90,000 men (Gibbon's traditional figure is 95,000), refusing allied embassies' offers of help but ordering the Armenian king Arsaces to muster. He built a fleet of over 1,000 ships at Samosata plus 50 pontoon ships, sent 30,000 men under Procopius and Sebastianus toward Media, and aimed apparently at regime change — replacing Shapur II with his brother Hormisdas. After taking Pirisabora in late April, the army reached Ctesiphon by mid-May and won a tactical victory, but the general Victor barred troops from entering the open gates; the generals persuaded Julian not to besiege the city, and he marched into the interior, ordering the fleet destroyed — a hasty decision, the page says, leaving him on the wrong side of the Tigris as the Persians flooded the land and burned food in his path. A council of war on 16 June 363 chose retreat northward to Corduene. Julian died of his wounds on 26 June 363 near Samarra, aged 31–32, and was buried at Tarsus. The report that his dying words were 'You have won, Galilean' is considered apocryphal, appearing in Theodoret in the 5th century."
  },
