   "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Julian_(emperor)",
   "title": "Julian (emperor) - Wikipedia",
   "snippet": "<strong>Julian</strong> (Latin: Flavius Claudius ... Roman <strong>emperor</strong> from 361 to 363, as well as a notable philosopher and author in Greek. His rejection of Christianity, public conversion to paganism, and promotion of Neoplatonic Hellenism caused him to be remembered as <strong>Julian</strong> <strong>the</strong> <strong>Apostate</strong> in the Christian ...",
   "text": "Julian (Flavius Claudius Julianus; born Constantinople 331, died 26 June 363 near Samarra, aged 31–32) was Caesar in the West from 355 and emperor from 361 to 363. In the turmoil after Constantine's death in 337, his cousin Constantius II, the page says, appears to have led a massacre of most of Julian's close relatives; Julian and his half-brother Gallus survived, likely saved by their youth, and were excluded from public life, strictly guarded, and given a Christian education. The page hedges that if Julian's later writings are to be believed, Constantius was later tormented with guilt over the massacre.\n\nJulian's conversion from Christianity to paganism came at about age 20; looking back in 362, Julian wrote that he had spent twenty years in the way of Christianity and twelve in the true way, that of Helios. He began studying Neoplatonism in Asia Minor in 351 under Aedesius, then under Aedesius' student Eusebius of Myndus, who told him of Maximus of Ephesus and his theurgy: Eusebius described Maximus making a statue of Hecate smile and laugh and her torches ignite, while insisting, in words the page quotes, that Julian \"must not marvel at any of these things, even as I marvel not, but rather believe that the thing of the highest importance is that purification of the soul which is attained by reason.\" Despite Eusebius' warnings against the \"impostures of witchcraft and magic that cheat the senses,\" Julian sought out Maximus; according to the historian Eunapius, Julian told his former teacher, \"farewell, and devote yourself to your books. You have shown me the man I was in search of.\"\n\nAfter Gallus was executed in 354 for a rule of terror, Julian was summoned to Constantius' court at Milan and held for a year under suspicion of treasonable intrigue; he was cleared partly because Empress Eusebia intervened (Julian thanks her in his third oration), though a cited article by Shaun Tougher argues the kind Eusebia of the panegyric is a literary creation acting on Constantius' behalf. Permitted to study in Athens, Julian knew Gregory of Nazianzus and Basil the Great there and was initiated into the Eleusinian Mysteries, which he later tried to restore. At the end of 357, with prestige from his victory over the Alamanni, he blocked a tax increase by the Gallic prefect Florentius and took personal charge of Belgica Secunda, his first civil administration; he and Florentius clashed repeatedly over taxation and bureaucratic corruption.\n\nIn February 360 Constantius ordered more than half of Julian's Gallic troops east, the order bypassing Julian; troops of the Petulantes mutinied and proclaimed Julian Augustus. Zosimus says army officers distributed an anonymous tract complaining of Constantius and fearing for Julian's fate; Ammianus Marcellinus even suggested Constantius sent the order out of fear of Julian's popularity, at Florentius' urging. In spring 361 Julian captured the Alamannic king Vadomarius, claiming he had been in league with Constantius, then advanced down the Danube; the page quotes Julian's statement of late November that he set out \"because, having been declared a public enemy, I meant to frighten him [Constantius] merely, and that our quarrel should result in intercourse on more friendly terms...\"\n\nAs emperor Julian idealized Hadrian and Marcus Aurelius and, in his first panegyric to Constantius, described the ideal ruler as essentially primus inter pares, operating under the same laws as his subjects; in Constantinople he regularly sat in the Senate, debating and speaking as one member among others. At Delphi he tried to restore the Castalian spring and, told the bones of the 3rd-century bishop Babylas were suppressing the god, ordered their removal — which the page itself calls a public-relations mistake, as it provoked a massive Christian procession; when the temple then burned, Julian suspected Christians, ordered stricter investigations than usual, and shut the city's chief Christian church before the fire proved accidental.\n\nOn 5 March 363, despite omens against the campaign, Julian left Antioch for Persia with about 65,000–83,000 or 80,000–90,000 men (the page gives both ranges; Gibbon's traditional figure is 95,000 effectives), supported by a fleet of over 1,000 ships built at Samosata plus 50 pontoon ships; his ultimate aim seems to have been regime change, replacing Shapur II with his brother Hormisdas. Reaching Ctesiphon by mid-May, the Romans won a tactical victory outside the walls, but the master-general Victor ordered the troops not to enter the open gates; a council of war persuaded Julian not to besiege the city, and he marched into the interior ordering the fleet destroyed — a decision the page calls hasty, leaving him on the wrong side of the Tigris as the Persians flooded the land and burned food in his path. A second council on 16 June 363 chose retreat northward to Corduene. Julian died of his wound on 26 June 363. His reported dying words, νενίκηκάς με, Γαλιλαῖε — Vicisti, Galilaee (\"You have won, Galilean\") — are flagged by the page as apocryphal, reported by Theodoret in the 5th century.\n\nOn religious policy: on 4 February 362 Julian promulgated an edict guaranteeing freedom of religion, declaring all religions equal before the law; the page reports it was seen as an act of favor toward the Jews in order to upset the Christians. His School Edict required all public teachers to be approved by the emperor; Ammianus Marcellinus explains it as intending to prevent Christian teachers from using the pagan texts at the core of classical education, and the page quotes the edict: \"If they want to learn literature, they have Luke and Mark: Let them go back to their churches and expound on them.\" The page notes the edict dealt a severe financial blow to Christian scholars and teachers by depriving them of students. His laws tended to target wealthy and educated Christians, aiming not to destroy Christianity but to drive it out of the governing classes. The pagan restoration foundered because temple funds had been seized by Constantine for the Church, civic leaders lacked money and motive, and communities were divided. Julian complained, in words the page quotes: \"These impious Galileans not only feed their own poor, but ours also; welcoming them into their agapae, they attract them, as children are attracted, with cakes,\" and he envisioned a Roman philanthropic system to reduce pagan reliance on Christian charity. The failure to rebuild the Jerusalem Temple may be ascribed to the Galilee earthquake of 363; Gregory Nazianzen's Orations describe the builders \"being driven against one another, as though by a furious blast of wind, and sudden heaving of the earth,\" with a flame issuing from a church where they sought refuge, which Gregory calls \"what all people nowadays report and believe\" — while Gibbon considered this unreliable, positing sabotage or accident, and divine intervention remains a common view among Christian historians.\n\nOn doctrine, the page quotes Julian accepting Plato's Timaeus: \"when Zeus was setting all things in order there fell from him drops of sacred blood, and from them, as they say, arose the race of men,\" and arguing against descent from a single pair, \"they who had the power to create one man and one woman only, were able to create many men and women at once...\", citing \"how very different in their bodies are the Germans and Scythians from the Libyans and Ethiopians.\" The Christian historian Socrates Scholasticus claims Julian believed himself Alexander the Great in another body, via transmigration of souls in accordance with Pythagoras and Plato."
  },
  {
   "url": "https://christianscholars.com/julian-against-christian-educators/",
   "title": "Julian against Christian Educators: Julian and Basil on a Proper Education - Christian Scholar’s Review",
   "snippet": "Julian sharpened this seemingly innocuous edict when he soon clarified that educators must advocate religious and moral values congruent with those transmitted by Greek literature and so, must “strive after the ancients’ reverence towards the gods.”3 Julian thus made clear that Christian educators had no place in the ‘schools’ of the empire, which were the required training grounds for participation and position in elite Roman society.4 · As “antiquity’s most notable pagan convert” from Christianity,5 Julian understood clearly Christianity’s threat to his program to restore paganism to the empire. In fact, Julian ‘the Apostate’ may have seen more clearly than his Christian contemporaries the subversive influence Christian educators had on his imperial program.",
   "text": "Benjamin D. Wayman's article for Christian Scholars Review (christianscholars.com) compares the emperor Julian's and Basil of Caesarea's approaches to Greek education in late antiquity, framed by the 'Constantinian shift' that moved fourth-century Christians from the margins of Roman society to power. Wayman reports that Constantine's support of Christianity was maintained by his sons but repudiated by his nephew Julian, who despite a Christian upbringing and service as a church lector, on becoming sole emperor in 361 publicly discarded Christianity and, in the words of the source Wayman cites, 'fervently embraced the gods of Greece and Rome,' attacking what he called the 'religion for the uneducated.' On 17 June 362, either before settling in Antioch or shortly after arrival, Julian issued his edict on education, opening with the injunction, 'School teachers and professors ought to be distinguished, first by character, and, second, by eloquence.' Wayman notes the edict seemed innocuous but that Christians were incensed: one scholar (Robert Browning, cited in a footnote) observes the edict 'aroused the fury of the leaders of the Christian Church' and that the hostile Christian historical tradition counted it principal among Julian's crimes; even the pagan historian Ammianus Marcellinus decried it as 'inhumane.' Gregory Nazianzen, in invectives composed shortly after Julian died, mocked him, per the footnote: 'Where did you get this idea, you the most vain and greedy of men, to rob the Christians of (Greek) literature?' A clarifying rescript the same summer, possibly in response to a request, made Christian educators the explicit target. Julian stated that a 'proper education' (παιδείαν ὀρθὴν) results only 'in a healthy condition of mind...a mind that has understanding and true opinions about things good and evil, honorable and base,' treating Christians as diseased and unfit to teach, though Christian youth were not to be barred from schools because they were 'too ignorant to know which way to turn...For we ought, I think, to teach, not punish, the demented.' Wayman summarizes Julian's three charges: Christian teachers were hypocrites teaching texts they disbelieved; Greek literature was divine and inspired, so disbelieving teachers must be motivated by greed 'for the sake of a few drachmae'; and, as Edward Watts observes, the law 'for the first time, specifically defined education in religious terms – in pagan religious terms,' making explicit a religious formation previously implicit. The rescript's key passage is given verbatim: 'If they are real interpreters [ἐξηγηταὶ] of the ancient classics, let them first strive after the ancients' reverence towards the gods. If, however, they think that those writers were in error with respect to the most honored gods, then let them go to the churches of the Galilaeans to expound Matthew and Luke, since you Galilaeans are obeying them when you ordain that men should refrain from temple worship.' Julian likely composed Against the Galilaeans in Antioch in late 362, made public in early 363; the treatise is lost and partially preserved by the hostile recorder Cyril of Alexandria. Scholars cited describe its theme as the belief that Christianity is antithetical to and damaging civilized life. Karl Olav Sandnes is quoted (in footnotes) that the claim sins could be washed away 'by merely being immersed in a bath was appalling' to Julian, and that Julian mocked baptism: 'For my part, I wish that your ears and your tongues might be \"born anew,\" [ἐξαναγεννηθῆναι] as you would say, as regards these things.' Against this, Wayman presents Basil of Caesarea's Address to Young Men on Reading Greek Literature, whose dating is contested — Philip Rousseau is agnostic; Robert Winn argues c.355-356 against the traditional late-370s dating. Basil counseled critical engagement: 'And just as in plucking the blooms from a rose-bed we avoid the thorns, so also in garnering from such writings whatever is useful, let us guard ourselves against what is harmful,' and 'But least of all shall we give attention to them when they narrate anything about the gods.' He called pagans 'stage-folk' (σκηνῆς), accused Greek writers of 'fabricating tales' (ψυχαγωγίας), and orators of the 'art of lying' (ψεύδεσθαι τέχνην), and held Christians 'shall doubtless learn all these things more thoroughly in our own literature,' treating pagan learning as 'a kind of rough sketch.' Frances Young is cited arguing Basil drew on an ancient commonplace (resembling Plutarch) rather than 'devising some peculiar Christian double-think.' For Basil, school was preparatory; the church and home were the central sites of moral formation, so Christians could read Homer as Christians. Wayman reports that fourth-century Christians did not acknowledge the danger their educators posed to Julian's program, and that their inability to grasp his rationale 'may have been strategic...But open admittance of such a strategy is not to be found in the literature.' Previous educational policy resumed when Julian died. Vasiliki Limberis is quoted (footnote) that by prohibiting Christians from teaching the classics, Julian turned paideia from a status marker into 'a tool to keep Christians on the periphery of Roman society.'"
  },
