   "url": "https://roman-empire.net/collapse/julian",
   "title": "Emperor Julian the Apostate - AD 332 - The Roman Empire",
   "snippet": "Following these classical tracks, Julian studied <strong>grammar and rhetoric</strong> until he was moved from Constantinople to Nicomedia by the emperor in AD 342. Constantius II evidently didn’t like the idea of a youth of Constantine’s blood being too ...",
   "text": "The roman-empire.net page on Emperor Julian (by site author Franco Cavazzi, who describes himself as an independent historian whose site has been cited in textbooks and mentioned in outlets including the New York Times and The Guardian) gives a summary biography. Note: the page's fact box lists Julian's name as 'Flavius Magnus Magnentius,' which appears to be an error carried over from the site's Magnentius entry; the body text identifies him as son of Julius Constantius, half-brother of Constantine the Great, and Basilina.\n\nThe page reports that Julian was born in AD 332 at Constantinople; his father was killed in AD 337 in the murders of Constantine's relatives by the three brother-emperors Constantine II, Constantius II and Constans, leaving Julian, his half-brother Gallus, Eutropia and Nepotianus as the only surviving relatives besides the emperors. Constantius II had him educated by the eunuch Mardonius in the classical tradition, then exiled him with Gallus to the fortress of Macellum in Cappadocia for six years, where he received a Christian education while his pagan interests continued. After Gallus's execution in AD 354, Julian was sent to Mediolanum, then permitted to study at Athens, and recalled in AD 355 to be made Caesar, married to the emperor's sister Helena, and sent to the Rhine. Despite military inexperience he recovered Colonia Agrippina by AD 356, defeated a larger Alemanni force near Argentorate in AD 357, and won further victories in 358 and 359; the page notes his tax cuts made him popular in Gaul and that Constantius II is believed to have considered having him assassinated. When Constantius demanded his best troops for the Persian war, the soldiers refused and hailed Julian emperor in February AD 360; the page says Julian was said to be reluctant, offering possible reasons without resolving them. After failed negotiations he marched east in AD 361 with about 3,000 men via the German forests to the lower Danube; Constantius died of illness in Cilicia, and Julian entered Constantinople in December AD 361, declaring himself pagan, executing some of Constantius's supporters and exiling others, though the page stresses the accession was not as bloody as that of Constantine's sons.\n\nOn his religious policy the page reports that the church lost financial privileges, Christians were excluded from teaching, Julian favoured the Jews and considered rebuilding the Temple at Jerusalem, but that his 'moderate and philosophical nature' prevented violent persecution, so his measures failed; the page itself argues a more ruthless ruler might have succeeded since much of the population remained pagan. In March AD 363 he left Antioch with sixty thousand men, reached Ctesiphon by June, judged his force too small to take it, and retreated; on 26 June AD 363 he was hit by an arrow in a skirmish, though the page records a rumour that he was stabbed by a Christian soldier, and died of the wound. Buried outside Tarsus as he wished, he was later exhumed and moved to Constantinople.\n\nIn its FAQ the page attributes to the fifth-century church historian Theodoret the report that Julian flung blood from his wound into the air and said: 'You have conquered, Galilean [Jesus]!' The FAQ also asserts, without naming a source, that he 'was murdered by an anonymous assassin in AD 363,' and that pagans were preferred for office while Christians were expelled from the army and barred from teaching classical literature and philosophy."
  },
  {
   "url": "https://edinburghuniversitypress.com/book-julian-the-apostate.html",
   "title": "Julian the Apostate",
   "snippet": "<strong>This study of the last pagan Roman emperor provides remarkable insight into the man and his times</strong>The figure of Julian demands the attention of historians. As the last pagan Roman Emperor, he provides a focus for studying the religious ...",
   "text": "This is the Edinburgh University Press product page for Shaun Tougher's *Julian the Apostate* (paperback published January 2007, in the series Debates and Documents in Ancient History; 224 pages; ISBN paperback 9780748618873, ebook 9781474473286). The publisher's description presents Julian as the last pagan Roman emperor and a focus for studying the religious, social and political transformations of the fourth-century empire. It states that Julian elicited sharply divided opinion from his contemporaries, largely polarised between pagan supporters and Christian opponents, and that modern literature on him is similarly divided; the page lists open questions the book poses, including whether Julian was a prospective saviour of the empire or out of touch and living in the past, whether he was an evangelist for Mithraism or a more traditional pagan, and whether he was a shrewd military man or a rash risk-taker whose luck ran out on his Persian expedition. The book is organised in two parts: Part I, 'Debates', comprises an introduction ('The Fascination of Julian'), chapters on Family, Conversion, Gaul, Emperor: Style and Reform, Religion, and Persia, and a conclusion ('The Elusiveness of Julian'). Part II, 'Documents', gathers translated primary texts: numerous works by Julian himself (panegyrics on Constantius II and the Empress Eusebia, the consolation on Salutius's departure, letters to Priscus, Oribasius, the Athenians, Maximus, Themistius, Bishop Aetius, the people of Alexandria and others, the Rescript on Christian Teachers, *The Caesars*, *To the Mother of the Gods*, *To King Helios*, *Misopogon*), plus inscriptions, extracts from the Theodosian Code, Aurelius Victor's *De Caesaribus*, Claudius Mamertinus's speech of thanks to Julian, a series of Libanius texts including the funeral oration for Julian and the autobiography, Salutius's *Concerning the Gods and the Universe*, and a Gregory of Nazianzus item (the contents listing is cut off mid-entry). The author bio states that Shaun Tougher is Professor of Late Roman and Byzantine History at Cardiff University, with a BA from Queen's University Belfast and a PhD from St Andrews, working on dynastic history and on gender and society (notably eunuchs and imperial women); his listed publications include *The Reign of Leo VI (886-912)* (1997), *Julian the Apostate* (2007), *The Eunuch in Byzantine History and Society* (2008), and *The Roman Castman: Eunuchs in the Roman Empire* (2021) — the page gives the title as *The Roman Castrati*. This page is a bookseller's summary, not testimony; everything in it is the publisher's framing of the book rather than a direct record of Julian."
  },
  {
   "url": "https://dokumen.pub/a-companion-to-julian-the-apostate-2019046065-2019046066-9789004414563-9789004416314.html",
   "title": "A Companion to Julian the Apostate 2019046065, 2019046066, 9789004414563, 9789004416314 - DOKUMEN.PUB",
   "snippet": "10: “Faites pour un moment abstraction des vérités révélées; cherchez dans toute la nature, et vous n’y trouverez pas de plus grand objet que les Antonins; Julian même, Julien (un suffrage ainsi arraché ne me rendra point complice de son apostasie), non, il n’y a point eu après lui de prince plus digne de gouverner les hommes”. On Montesquieu, Voltaire and Diderot see Spink, “Julian the Apostate in the Enlightenment” (→ i.16); Boch, Apostat ou philosophe? (→ i.16), pp. 503-680 and passim. 6 Diderot’s views on Julian can be found in no. XLIII of his Pensées philosophiques (1746) and in the article Éclectisme written for volume V of the famous Encyclopédie ou Dictionnaire raisonné des sciences, des arts et des métiers (1755).",
   "text": "110 111 112 113 114 115 116\n\nI cite the translation of Ephraem in Lieu, The Emperor Julian (→ i.3), pp. 125-126. Griffith, “Ephraem the Syrian’s Hymns ‘Against Julian’” (→ iii.4), pp. 250-251 and 258-260. Shepardson, “Paschal Politics: Deploying the Temple’s Destruction against FourthCentury Judaizers”, p. 244. Shepardson, “Paschal Politics: Deploying the Temple’s Destruction against FourthCentury Judaizers”, pp. 233-260.\n\nIndex (compiled by Sabina Walter and Bernhard Kremer) All dates are AD unless otherwise indicated. Achilles, Greek demigod (altar of) 64, 110, 213 Aedesius, Greek philosopher (Neoplatonist, † 355) 328 Aegeae 219 n. 19, 267 n. 1 Aelius Aristides, Greek sophist († 181) 38 Africa, province of 23, 24 n. 47, 106, 131 n. 24, 138 n. 55, 139 n. 59, 141, 147, 158, 196, 210, 215, 219 n. 18, 328 Agri Decumates 78 n. 33, 83, 87 Alamanni 66–87, 89 n. 60, 91, 94–5, 118 (Lentienses), 298 n. 22 Alexander the Great (356–323 BC) 7, 9, 249 n. 20, 268, 297 n. 18, 299, 306, 309, 370, 371 n. 36, 400 Alexander of Lycopolis, Greek philosopher (Platonist, 3rd c.) 212, 213 n. 9 Alexander Severus, Roman emperor 222–35 34, 138 n. 56 Alexandria (Egypt) 7, 132 n. 29, 156–8, 175 n. 6 + 9, 214 n. 11, 220–1, 336, 346 n. 46, 348, 383, 412 Alypius of Antioch, comes in charge of rebuilding the temple in Jerusalem 157, 271 Ambrose of Milan, bishop and Latin theologian († 397) 188 n. 44, 220, 280, 361, 367 Amelius, Greek philosopher (Neoplatonist, 3rd c.) 258 Amida 320, 365, 390 Ammianus Marcellinus, Latin historian († c. 395) 270–1, 294–312, 314–22 Amorgus 34 Anatha 300-4, 316, 322 Anatolius of Berytus, praetorian prefect of Illyricum 357–60 328 Ancyra 180 n. 21, 220, 367, 378, 384–5, 388, 390 Antioch (Syria) 7, 9, 11, 55, 125, 131 n. 24, 133–5, 138 n. 55, 143–4, 150, 156, 160 n. 127,\n\n162, 166–7, 174–7, 180, 219, 222–4, 245, 267, 269–71, 285–6, 297, 309, 335–41, 345–6, 349, 356, 366–7, 377–8, 383–6, 388, 390–1 Antisthenes, Greek philosopher (Cynic, † 365 BC) 48 Ant