   "snippet": "<strong>This study of the last pagan Roman emperor provides remarkable insight into the man and his times</strong>The figure of Julian...",
   "text": "The page is a partial scan of a scholarly sourcebook on Julian the Apostate (reprint ed., ISBN 9780895556325), and most of what survives in it is apparatus: a table of contents listing primary texts (Julian's letters and orations, Libanius, Ammianus, Gregory of Nazianzus, Ephrem, the church historians), a fragmentary chronology of Julian's life, a bibliography, and a long index. The substantive prose that remains comes from the volume's commentary. The commentary reports that Julian's Letter to the Athenians sketches his military record in Gaul: he describes the devastated condition of the province on his arrival, with Germans having sacked towns and settled near them; he celebrates his recovery of Cologne and especially the battle of Strasbourg in 357, which he calls a famous victory; and he claims Gaul was transformed, the barbarians driven out, towns recovered, and the Rhine reopened to Roman shipping. He records going on the offensive against the Salii and Chamavi in 358, and ties his economic success to his military one, noting that he abandoned the policy—devised by the Praetorian Prefect Florentius—of paying barbarians for passage on the Rhine, and that he opposed Florentius' greed. The commentary says this self-presentation is largely supported by other sources, and identifies Ammianus Marcellinus, who served in Gaul under Ursicinus in 355–7, as the major testimony: Ammianus describes Julian's recovery of Brumath, the assault on Alamanni sheltering on Rhine islands, the Strasbourg battle as an extended set piece, and the 357–8 campaigns against Alamanni and Franks across the Rhine, while fleshing out Julian's thin account of his financial measures against Florentius. The commentary notes it is unknowable whether Julian mentioned being hailed Augustus by the army after Strasbourg. A second passage discusses Julian's models, Alexander the Great and Trajan, as possible explanations for his Persian ambitions. It reports that the church historian Socrates alleges Julian believed he possessed Alexander's soul—flagging that this may be Christian mockery, akin to Gregory of Nazianzus' story that Julian wanted to throw himself into the Tigris so his body would vanish and he would be thought divine, a story also told of Alexander. Julian's own The Caesars, written at Antioch in 362, has the gods include Alexander among six finalists in a contest of rulers, with Trajan at the end flocking to Alexander as his inspiration. Among moderns, the commentary reports, Athanassiadi and Baynes argue strongly for Alexander's influence on Julian, Smith is unconvinced, and Lane Fox argues Julian viewed Alexander dispassionately, recognized his flaws, and that an interest in Alexander was normal for an emperor concerned with Persia, citing the Itinerary of Alexander written for Constantius II."
  },
  {
   "url": "https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2007/2007.09.36",
   "title": "Julian the Apostate. Debates and Documents in Ancient History series – Bryn Mawr Classical Review",
   "snippet": "3). Chapters 4 and 5, while still in line with the general chronology, are more thematic in character: they discuss the ‘imperial style’ of Julian (Ch. 4) and his religion (Ch. 5). Accompanying the volume’s historical overview of Julian are a timeline, a family tree of the emperor, and three maps of the Empire; a guided ‘further reading’ section, a section with ‘essay questions and exercise topics’, and a list of Internet resources.",
   "text": "This page is a review by Sergio Knipe of the University of Cambridge, published in Bryn Mawr Classical Review (BMCR 2007.09.36), of Shaun Tougher's 'Julian the Apostate' (Edinburgh University Press, 2007, xiii + 201 pages, ISBN 0748618864, £19.99 paperback), a volume in the Debates and Documents in Ancient History series. Knipe opens by noting, citing W. E. Kaegi's 1993 lament over a shortage of Julian studies, that by 2007 the previous decade had instead seen an extraordinary flourishing of work on the emperor, from academic biographies (he names Bringmann 2004 and Rosen 2006) to novels. Knipe reports that Tougher, a Cardiff University lecturer with three prior articles on Julian, describes his own book as an attempt to indicate the different views of Julian that exist and to supply a selection of the key evidence used in constructing those divergent interpretations. The book has two parts: Part I (pages 1-73), 'Debates', six chronologically arranged chapters covering Julian's childhood, his 'Conversion', the Gallic campaigns, his 'imperial style', his religion, and the Persian campaign; Part II (pages 74-177), 'Documents', primary texts in translation cross-referenced from Part I, plus a timeline, family tree, three maps, further reading, essay questions, and internet resources. Knipe reports that Tougher encourages readers 'to make up their own minds' (11) and concedes he can give only 'a partial account' (31); Tougher presents Julian variously as a curious philosophy student and as 'a super soldier and an economic whiz kid' (33), stressing the revolutionary character of his economic policy in Gaul and court-finance reform, which Knipe says reveals Julian's 'practical streak' (46). Knipe praises the questions raised about Julian's accession and the Persian campaign, but criticises the selection of issues: Tougher treats 'imperial style' without mentioning Julian's political philosophy, dwells on the 'Success or failure?' question (59-62), yet omits Julian's eccentricity regarding divination and any critical engagement with his organisation of the pagan clergy. Knipe attributes these gaps partly to bibliographic omissions reflecting, in his judgement, a 'decidedly anglophone prejudice': Tougher describes Julian's usurpation as possibly the product of a 'pagan conspiracy' without citing Bouffartigue's 1998 article, follows Asmus (1896) in presenting Julian's letters to priests as an effort to educate the clergy of a new 'pagan Church' (58) without addressing Mario Mazza's argument that their purpose is far less obvious, and ignores Peter van Nuffeln's suggestion that the Letter to Arsacius may be a fifth-century fabrication. Part II's sources include Julian himself, Libanius, Eunapius, Ammianus, Zosimus, Eutropius, Festus, Mamertinus' 362 panegyric, excerpts from the Theodosian Code, four inscriptions, Christian authors, three coin types, and the Louvre statue; missing, Knipe notes, are Julian's oration Against the Uneducated Cynics and the Ma'ayan Barukh inscription praising Julian as restaurator templorum. Knipe regrets that Tougher mostly reprints standard English translations — Socrates from 1890 and Philostorgius from 1855 — freshly translating only Orosius, with a few adaptations of his own for Julian's writings. His overall verdict is that the book raises questions rather than answering them and serves inexperienced readers, but as an unambitious introduction it offers little not already found in standard works such as Cameron's 'The Later Roman Empire' and Hunt's chapter in Cambridge Ancient History 13."
  }
