 "formation": [
  {
   "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Julian_(emperor)",
   "title": "Julian (emperor) - Wikipedia",
   "snippet": "↑ David Sear, Roman Coins and Their Values, <strong>Volume</strong> <strong>5</strong> (<strong>London</strong>: <strong>Spink</strong>, <strong>2014</strong>), p.",
   "text": "Julian — Flavius Claudius Julianus, Greek Ioulianos — was born in Constantinople in 331, son of Julius Constantius and Basilina, and died 26 June 363 near Samarra in Mesopotamia, aged 31–32. He was Caesar in the West from 6 November 355, was proclaimed Augustus by his troops in February 360, and reigned as sole emperor from 3 November 361 until his death. He married Helena in 355; she died in 360. The article reports that his rejection of Christianity and promotion of Neoplatonic Hellenism earned him the Christian-era name Julian the Apostate.\n\nThe article reports that in the turmoil after Constantine's death in 337, Julian's cousin Constantius II \"appears to have led\" a massacre of most of Julian's close relatives — descendants of the second marriage of Constantius Chlorus and Theodora — leaving Julian and his half-brother Constantius Gallus among the few surviving male relatives, \"likely saved by their youth.\" The two boys were excluded from public life, strictly guarded, and given a Christian education. The article hedges that \"if Julian's later writings are to be believed,\" Constantius was later tormented by guilt over the massacre of 337.\n\nJulian's conversion from Christianity to paganism occurred around age 20. Looking back in 362, Julian wrote that he had spent twenty years in the way of Christianity and twelve in the true way, that of Helios. He began studying Neoplatonism in Asia Minor in 351, first under the philosopher Aedesius, then under Aedesius' student Eusebius of Myndus. It was Eusebius who told Julian of Maximus of Ephesus, whose more mystical, theurgic Neoplatonism Eusebius himself criticized: Eusebius related that Maximus had invited him into the temple of Hecate and, chanting a hymn, made the goddess's statue smile and laugh and her torches ignite. Eusebius reportedly told Julian he \"must not marvel at any of these things, even as I marvel not, but rather believe that the thing of the highest importance is that purification of the soul which is attained by reason,\" and warned against the \"impostures of witchcraft and magic that cheat the senses.\" Despite this, Julian sought out Maximus as his new mentor; according to the historian Eunapius, Julian's parting words to Eusebius were \"farewell, and devote yourself to your books. You have shown me the man I was in search of.\"\n\nThe dynastic background: Constantine II died in 340 attacking his brother Constans; Constans fell in 350 against the usurper Magnentius, leaving Constantius II sole emperor. In 351 Constantius made Gallus caesar of the East; in 354 Gallus, who had imposed a rule of terror, was executed. Julian was summoned to Constantius' court at Mediolanum in 354 and held for a year under suspicion of treasonable intrigue, first with his brother and then with Claudius Silvanus. He was cleared, in part because Empress Eusebia intervened for him — Julian expresses gratitude to her in his third oration — and was permitted to study in Athens. There he became acquainted with two future bishops and saints, Gregory of Nazianzus and Basil the Great, and in the same period was initiated into the Eleusinian Mysteries, which he would later try to restore.\n\nAs Caesar in Gaul, Julian's first priority was driving out the barbarians who had breached the Rhine, but the article reports that at the end of 357, confident from his victory over the Alamanni, he blocked a tax increase by the Gallic praetorian prefect Florentius and personally took charge of Belgica Secunda — his first experience of civil administration, a role properly belonging to the prefect, and one in which his views were shaped by his liberal education in Greece. He clashed repeatedly with Florentius over taxes and over Florentius' corruption.\n\nIn February 360, after Shapur II took Amida, Constantius ordered more than half of Julian's Gallic troops east, addressing the order directly to the commanders and bypassing Julian. Julian at first tried to expedite it, but troops of the Petulantes, unwilling to leave Gaul, mutinied and acclaimed him emperor. Zosimus reports that army officers distributed an anonymous tract voicing complaints against Constantius and fear for Julian's fate; the prefect Florentius was notably absent, organizing supplies at Vienne, and Julian later blamed him for the order's arrival. Ammianus Marcellinus even suggested Constantius sent the order out of fear of Julian's growing popularity, at Florentius' urging. In spring 361 Julian captured the Alamannic king Vadomarius, claiming he had been in league with Constantius, then advanced down the Danube while his general Nevitta secured the pass of Succi. Julian stated in late November that he had set out \"because, having been declared a public enemy, I meant to frighten him [Constantius] merely, and that our quarrel should result in intercourse on more friendly terms.\"\n\nAs emperor, the article reports, Julian idealized Hadrian and Marcus Aurelius; in his first panegyric to Constantius he described the ideal ruler as primus inter pares, \"first among equals,\" subject to the same laws as his subjects, and in Constantinople he regularly participated in Senate debates as if one among the members. On religion, the article summarizes that he aimed not to destroy Christianity but to drive it out of \"the governing classes of the empire.\" On 4 February 362 he promulgated an edict of religious freedom, which the article notes was seen as favoring the Jews to upset the Christians. His School Edict required imperial approval of public teachers; Ammianus explains it as barring Christian teachers from pagan texts, and the edict itself says, \"If they want to learn literature, they have Luke and Mark: Let them go back to their churches and expound on them.\" Envious of Christian charity's reach, he envisioned a pagan philanthropic system, writing: \"These impious Galileans not only feed their own poor, but ours also; welcoming them into their agapae, they attract them, as children are attracted, with cakes.\" His Neoplatonism shaped his anthropology: he wrote that \"when Zeus was setting all things in order there fell from him drops of sacred blood, and from them, as they say, arose the race of men,\" and argued against a single-pair origin of humanity, asking \"how very different in their bodies are the Germans and Scythians from the Libyans and Ethiopians.\" Socrates Scholasticus claims Julian believed himself Alexander the Great \"in another body,\" per Pythagorean and Platonic transmigration. At Antioch he tried to restore the Castalian spring at Delphi's Apollo temple and ordered the removal of bishop Babylas' bones, provoking a massive Christian procession; when the temple then burned, he suspected Christians, closed the city's chief church, and ordered investigations that in the end showed the fire was accidental.\n\nOn 5 March 363, despite adverse omens, Julian left Antioch for Persia with forces the sources give variously as 65,000–83,000, 80,000–90,000, or the 95,000 accepted by Gibbon. After a tactical victory before Ctesiphon, his generals persuaded him not to besiege the city; he burned his fleet and struck inland, was forced into retreat, and died of a wound on 26 June 363. The article flags as apocryphal the report — from Theodoret's 5th-century Historia ecclesiastica — that his dying words were \"Vicisti, Galilaee\" (\"You have won, Galilean\"). On the failed rebuilding of the Jerusalem Temple, Gregory Nazianzen describes the builders driven apart by wind and earth-heaving and a flame issuing from a church, adding that this is \"what all people nowadays report and believe\"; Gibbon considered the account unreliable, favoring sabotage or accident."
