   "snippet": "<strong>Julian</strong> (Latin: Flavius Claudius ... Roman <strong>emperor</strong> from 361 to 363, as well as a notable philosopher and author in Greek. His rejection of Christianity, public conversion to paganism, and promotion of Neoplatonic Hellenism caused him to be remembered as <strong>Julian</strong> <strong>the</strong> <strong>Apostate</strong> in the Christian ...",
   "text": "The Wikipedia reference page (a tertiary summary drawing on Ammianus Marcellinus, Zosimus, Eunapius and Julian's own writings) gives Julian's full name as Flavius Claudius Julianus, born in 331 in Constantinople, son of Julius Constantius and Basilina, of the Constantinian dynasty; he married Helena in 355 and she died in 360. The page reports that in the turmoil after Constantine's death in 337, Julian's cousin Constantius II \"appears to have led\" a massacre of most of Julian's close relatives — allegedly ordering the murders of many descendants of the second marriage of Constantius Chlorus and Theodora — leaving as surviving male relatives only Constantius II and his brothers Constantine II and Constans I, plus the cousins Julian and Constantius Gallus, Julian's half-brother. Julian and Gallus were excluded from public life, strictly guarded in their youth, and given a Christian education; the page says they were \"likely saved by their youth,\" and adds the hedge that \"if Julian's later writings are to be believed,\" Constantius was later tormented with guilt over the massacre of 337.\n\nThe page dates Julian's conversion from Christianity to paganism to around age 20, and records that Julian, looking back in 362, wrote that he had spent twenty years in the way of Christianity and twelve in the true way, that of Helios. He began studying Neoplatonism in Asia Minor in 351, first under the philosopher Aedesius, then under Aedesius' student Eusebius of Myndus. It was from Eusebius that Julian learned of Maximus of Ephesus, whose more mystical, theurgic Neoplatonism Eusebius criticized: Eusebius related being invited by Maximus into the temple of Hecate, where the theurgist, chanting a hymn, caused the goddess's statue to smile and laugh and her torches to ignite. Eusebius reportedly told Julian he \"must not marvel at any of these things, even as I marvel not, but rather believe that the thing of the highest importance is that purification of the soul which is attained by reason,\" and warned against the \"impostures of witchcraft and magic that cheat the senses\" and \"the works of conjurers who are insane men led astray into the exercise of earthly and material powers.\" Despite the warnings Julian was intrigued and sought out Maximus as his new mentor; according to the historian Eunapius, on leaving Eusebius he told his former teacher: \"farewell, and devote yourself to your books. You have shown me the man I was in search of.\"\n\nThe page then traces the dynastic attrition that shaped his path: Constantine II died in 340 attacking his brother Constans; Constans fell in 350 in the war against the usurper Magnentius, leaving Constantius II sole emperor. In 351 Constantius made Gallus caesar of the East while he dealt with Magnentius, whom he defeated that year; in 354 Gallus, who had imposed a rule of terror, was executed. Julian was summoned to Constantius' court at Mediolanum in 354 and held for a year under suspicion of treasonable intrigue — first in connection with his brother, then with Claudius Silvanus — and was cleared in part because Empress Eusebia intervened on his behalf; the page notes Julian expresses gratitude to the empress in his third oration, but also cites Shaun Tougher's argument (The Classical Quarterly, 1998) that the kindly Eusebia of that panegyric is a literary creation and that she was doing her husband's bidding in bringing Julian around to what Constantius wanted of him. After his clearance Julian was permitted to study in Athens, where he became acquainted with Gregory of Nazianzus and Basil the Great, both later bishops and saints, and in the same period was initiated into the Eleusinian Mysteries, which he would later try to restore.\n\nAs Caesar in Gaul from 355, the page records, Julian's first experience of civil administration came at the end of 357, when, with the prestige of his victory over the Alamanni, he prevented a tax increase by the Gallic praetorian prefect Florentius and personally took charge of Belgica Secunda; his views were influenced by his liberal education in Greece, and he clashed repeatedly with Florentius over taxation and bureaucratic corruption. In February 360, after Shapur II took Amida, Constantius ordered more than half of Julian's Gallic troops east, bypassing Julian to address the commanders directly; troops of the Petulantes mutinied, and per Zosimus the officers distributed an anonymous tract complaining of Constantius and fearing for Julian's fate, while Florentius was notably absent in Vienne — Julian later blamed him for the order's arrival, and Ammianus suggested Constantius sent it out of fear of Julian's popularity, on Florentius' urging. In spring 361 Julian captured the Alamannic king Vadomarius, claiming he had been in league with Constantius, then advanced down the Danube; the page quotes Julian's statement in late November that he set out \"because, having been declared a public enemy, I meant to frighten him [Constantius] merely, and that our quarrel should result in intercourse on more friendly terms...\" — hedged as Julian's own later account of his motives."
