 "testimony": [
  {
   "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Julian_(emperor)",
   "title": "Julian (emperor) - Wikipedia",
   "snippet": "↑ David Sear, Roman Coins and Their Values, <strong>Volume</strong> <strong>5</strong> (<strong>London</strong>: <strong>Spink</strong>, <strong>2014</strong>), p.",
   "text": "Julian (Flavius Claudius Julianus), born 331 in Constantinople, was Caesar in the West from 355 to 360 and emperor from 3 November 361 until his death on 26 June 363, aged 31–32, near Samarra. After Constantine's death in 337, his cousin Constantius II appears to have led a massacre of Julian's close relatives — the article hedges that Constantius \"allegedly ordered\" the murders and that Julian and his half-brother Gallus were likely saved by their youth; it adds that if Julian's later writings are to be believed, Constantius was afterward tormented with guilt. Julian's conversion to paganism came around age 20: looking back in 362, Julian wrote that he had spent twenty years in the way of Christianity and twelve in the true way, that of Helios. He began studying Neoplatonism in Asia Minor in 351 under Aedesius, then under Eusebius of Myndus, from whom he learned of Maximus of Ephesus. Eusebius described how Maximus, chanting a hymn in the temple of Hecate, made the goddess's statue smile and her torches ignite, but told Julian he \"must not marvel at any of these things, even as I marvel not, but rather believe that the thing of the highest importance is that purification of the soul which is attained by reason,\" warning against the \"impostures of witchcraft and magic that cheat the senses.\" Julian was intrigued anyway; according to the historian Eunapius, on leaving Eusebius he told him: \"farewell, and devote yourself to your books. You have shown me the man I was in search of.\"\n\nIn 354, after Gallus's execution for a rule of terror, Julian was summoned to Constantius's court at Milan and held for a year under suspicion of treasonable intrigue; he was cleared partly because Empress Eusebia intervened, and Julian expresses gratitude to her in his third oration — though the article notes Shaun Tougher's argument that the kind Eusebia of the panegyric is a literary creation acting at her husband's bidding. Permitted to study in Athens, Julian knew Gregory of Nazianzus and Basil the Great there, and was initiated into the Eleusinian Mysteries, which he later tried to restore.\n\nAt the end of 357, emboldened by his victory over the Alamanni, Julian blocked a tax increase by the Gallic praetorian prefect Florentius and personally took charge of Belgica Secunda — properly the prefect's role — clashing with Florentius over taxes and bureaucratic corruption. In February 360 Constantius ordered more than half of Julian's Gallic troops east, bypassing Julian to address the commanders directly; troops of the Petulantes mutinied. According to Zosimus, army officers distributed an anonymous tract of complaints against Constantius; Florentius was notably absent, organizing supplies in Vienne, and Julian later blamed him for the order's arrival. Ammianus Marcellinus even suggested Constantius sent the order, at Florentius's urging, out of fear of Julian's growing popularity. In spring 361 Julian captured the Alamannic king Vadomarius, claiming he had been in league with Constantius; he then divided his forces, sent Nevitta to secure the pass of Succi, and moved down the Danube toward civil war, stating in late November that he set out \"because, having been declared a public enemy, I meant to frighten him [Constantius] merely, and that our quarrel should result in intercourse on more friendly terms...\"\n\nAs emperor he blamed Constantine for the state of the administration, idealized Hadrian and Marcus Aurelius, and in his first panegyric to Constantius described the ideal ruler as primus inter pares; in Constantinople he sat in the Senate debating and speaking as one member among others. At Delphi he tried to revive the Castalian spring; advised that the bones of bishop Babylas were suppressing the god, he ordered their removal — a public-relations mistake that drew a massive Christian procession — and when the temple then burned he suspected Christians, ordered stricter investigations, and shut the city's chief church before the fire proved accidental.\n\nOn 5 March 363, despite adverse omens, Julian left Antioch with about 65,000–83,000 or 80,000–90,000 men (Gibbon's traditional figure is 95,000), declined offers of assistance from small powers but ordered the Armenian king Arsaces to muster, and sent 30,000 men under Procopius and Sebastianus toward Media. He had built a fleet of over 1,000 ships at Samosata plus 50 pontoon ships; his ultimate aim seems to have been \"regime change,\" replacing Shapur II with his brother Hormisdas. The army reached Circesium in early April, passed Dura on 6 April, and took Pirisabora at the end of April; the Sassanids broke the dikes, flooding the land. By mid-May the army reached Ctesiphon and won a tactical victory before its gates, but the master-general Victor, fearing encirclement, ordered the troops not to pursue through the open gates. A council of war persuaded Julian not to besiege the city; he marched into the Persian interior and ordered the fleet destroyed, a decision the article calls hasty, since the army was on the wrong side of the Tigris without siege equipment or clear retreat. A second council on 16 June 363 resolved to withdraw northward to Corduene. The report that his dying words were νενίκηκάς με, Γαλιλαῖε (\"You have won, Galilean\") is considered apocryphal; it comes from Theodoret's Historia ecclesiastica in the 5th century and was later used by Swinburne (1866) and Krasiński (1833).\n\nOn doctrine, the article quotes Julian writing, following Plato's Timaeus, \"when Zeus was setting all things in order there fell from him drops of sacred blood, and from them, as they say, arose the race of men,\" and \"they who had the power to create one man and one woman only, were able to create many men and women at once...\"; against the single-pair origin he asked, \"how very different in their bodies are the Germans and Scythians from the Libyans and Ethiopians.\" Socrates Scholasticus claims Julian believed himself Alexander the Great \"in another body\" by transmigration of souls. Julian's religious reformation aimed not to destroy Christianity but to drive it out of \"the governing classes of the empire,\" and his laws tended to target wealthy, educated Christians. On 4 February 362 he promulgated an edict guaranteeing freedom of religion, declaring all religions equal before the law; the article reports it was seen as a favor to the Jews meant to upset Christians. His School Edict required imperial approval of public teachers; Ammianus Marcellinus explains it as intended to bar Christian teachers from pagan texts, and the edict itself says, \"If they want to learn literature, they have Luke and Mark: Let them go back to their churches and expound on them\" (the surrounding interpretive claims carry citation-needed flags). The edict deprived Christian teachers of students and income. Julian found temple funds had been seized by Constantine to support the Church, and civic leaders had neither funds nor motive to revive festivals. Seeking to counter Christian charity, he wrote: \"These impious Galileans not only feed their own poor, but ours also; welcoming them into their agapae, they attract them, as children are attracted, with cakes.\" His attempt to rebuild the Jerusalem Temple failed, a failure ascribed to the Galilee earthquake of 363; in his Orations, Gregory Nazianzen described the builders as \"being driven against one another, as though by a furious blast of wind, and sudden heaving of the earth,\" with some fleeing to a church where \"a flame issued forth... and stopped them,\" adding that this is \"what all people nowadays report and believe.\" Gibbon considered Gregory's account unreliable, positing sabotage or accident, while divine intervention remains a common view among Christian historians."
  },
