   "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Julian_(emperor)",
   "title": "Julian (emperor) - Wikipedia",
   "snippet": "<strong>Julian</strong> (Latin: Flavius Claudius ... Roman <strong>emperor</strong> from 361 to 363, as well as a notable philosopher and author in Greek. His rejection of Christianity, public conversion to paganism, and promotion of Neoplatonic Hellenism caused him to be remembered as <strong>Julian</strong> <strong>the</strong> <strong>Apostate</strong> in the Christian ...",
   "text": "Julian (Flavius Claudius Julianus; Greek Ἰουλιανός), born 331 in Constantinople, died 26 June 363 near Samarra in Mesopotamia, was Caesar of the West from 6 November 355, was proclaimed Augustus by his troops in February 360, and reigned as sole emperor from 3 November 361 until his death; he was buried at Tarsus and later at the Church of the Holy Apostles. His father was Julius Constantius, his mother Basilina; he married Constantius II's sister Helena in 355 (she died 360). A philosopher and author in Greek, he rejected Christianity for Neoplatonic paganism and is remembered in Christian tradition as Julian the Apostate.\n\nAfter Constantine's death in 337, Julian's cousin Constantius II appears to have led a massacre of most of Julian's close relatives; Julian and his half-brother Gallus survived, the page says, likely because of their youth, and were strictly guarded and given a Christian education. The page hedges that if Julian's later writings are to be believed, Constantius was afterward tormented by guilt over the massacre. Julian converted to paganism around age twenty; looking back in 362 he wrote that he had spent twenty years in the way of Christianity and twelve in the true way, that of Helios. He began studying Neoplatonism in Asia Minor in 351 under Aedesius and then Eusebius of Myndus, who told him of Maximus of Ephesus: in the temple of Hecate, Maximus chanted a hymn and caused the goddess's statue to smile and laugh and her torches to ignite. Eusebius reportedly told Julian he \"must not marvel at any of these things, even as I marvel not, but rather believe that the thing of the highest importance is that purification of the soul which is attained by reason,\" and warned against the \"impostures of witchcraft and magic that cheat the senses.\" Julian nonetheless sought out Maximus, and according to the historian Eunapius told Eusebius on leaving: \"farewell, and devote yourself to your books. You have shown me the man I was in search of.\"\n\nConstantine II died in 340 attacking Constans; Constans fell in 350 against Magnentius, leaving Constantius II sole emperor. Gallus was made caesar of the East in 351 and executed in 354 after a rule of terror. Julian was summoned to court at Mediolanum in 354 and held a year under suspicion of treasonable intrigue, cleared partly through Empress Eusebia's intervention — Julian expresses gratitude to her in his third oration, though a footnote cites Shaun Tougher arguing the kind Eusebia of that panegyric is a literary creation and that she was doing Constantius's bidding. Permitted to study in Athens, Julian knew Gregory of Nazianzus and Basil the Great there and was initiated into the Eleusinian Mysteries, which he later tried to restore.\n\nAt the end of 357, confident from victory over the Alamanni, Julian blocked a tax increase by the Gallic praetorian prefect Florentius and personally took charge of Belgica Secunda, clashing repeatedly with Florentius over taxation and bureaucratic corruption. In February 360 Constantius ordered more than half of Julian's Gallic troops east, bypassing Julian to address the commanders directly; the Petulantes mutinied and proclaimed Julian emperor at Paris. Zosimus says officers distributed an anonymous tract complaining of Constantius and fearing for Julian's fate; Florentius was notably absent, organizing supplies at Vienne, and Julian later blamed him for the order's arrival; Ammianus Marcellinus even suggested Constantius sent it on Florentius's urging out of fear of Julian's popularity. In spring 361 Julian captured the Alamanni king Vadomarius, claiming he had been in league with Constantius, then advanced down the Danube; his general Nevitta secured the pass of Succi. Julian stated in late November that he had set out \"because, having been declared a public enemy, I meant to frighten him [Constantius] merely, and that our quarrel should result in intercourse on more friendly terms.\"\n\nAs emperor Julian blamed Constantine for the state of the administration, idealized Hadrian and Marcus Aurelius, and in his first panegyric to Constantius described the ideal ruler as primus inter pares, operating under the same laws as his subjects; in Constantinople he regularly attended and debated in the Senate. At Delphi he tried to revive the Castalian spring; advised that the bones of the third-century bishop Babylas were suppressing the god, he ordered their removal, provoking a massive Christian procession, and when the temple then burned he suspected Christians, ordered unusually strict investigations, and shut the city's chief Christian church before the fire was proved accidental.\n\nOn 5 March 363, despite adverse omens, Julian left Antioch for Persia with about 65,000–83,000 or 80,000–90,000 men (the page notes Gibbon's traditional figure of 95,000). He ordered the Armenian king Arsaces to muster an army, crossed the Euphrates near Hierapolis, sent 30,000 men under Procopius and Sebastianus eastward toward Media, and had built at Samosata a fleet of over 1,000 supply ships plus 50 pontoon ships; his ultimate aim seems to have been replacing Shapur II with his brother Hormisdas. Turning south at Circesium in early April, passing Dura on 6 April, and taking the fortress of Pirisabora in late April, the army reached Ctesiphon by mid-May, crossed the Tigris by night, and won a tactical victory before the walls, but the general Victor ordered the troops not to pursue through the open gates. A council of war persuaded Julian not to besiege the city; marching east in hope of joining Procopius, he ordered the fleet destroyed — a decision the page calls hasty — and a second council on 16 June 363 resolved to retreat north through Corduene. Julian died of a wound on 26 June 363. His dying words are reported as νενίκηκάς με, Γαλιλαῖε (\"You have won, Galilean\"), but the page marks this apocryphal, attested in Theodoret's Historia ecclesiastica (3.25) in the fifth century.\n\nOn religion: Julian accepted the creation account of Plato's Timaeus, writing that \"when Zeus was setting all things in order there fell from him drops of sacred blood, and from them, as they say, arose the race of men,\" and that \"they who had the power to create one man and one woman only, were able to create many men and women at once,\" arguing against a single-pair origin by asking \"how very different in their bodies are the Germans and Scythians from the Libyans and Ethiopians.\" Socrates Scholasticus claims Julian believed himself Alexander the Great \"in another body\" through transmigration of souls. His anti-Christian measures targeted the wealthy and educated, aiming not to destroy Christianity but to drive it from \"the governing classes of the empire.\" On 4 February 362 he promulgated an edict of religious freedom declaring all religions equal before the law, which the page says was seen as favoring the Jews to upset the Christians. His School Edict required imperial approval of public teachers; Ammianus explains it as barring Christian teachers from pagan texts, and the page quotes the edict — flagging citation needed — as saying: \"If they want to learn literature, they have Luke and Mark: Let them go back to their churches and expound on them.\" Finding temple funds seized under Constantine and civic leaders unwilling to revive festivals, Julian also tried to build a pagan philanthropic system to counter Christian charity, writing: \"These impious Galileans not only feed their own poor, but ours also; welcoming them into their agapae, they attract them, as children are attracted, with cakes.\" The failure to rebuild the Jerusalem Temple is ascribed to the Galilee earthquake of 363; Gregory Nazianzen's Orations describe builders driven apart as by wind and earth-heaving and stopped by a flame issuing from a church, which Gregory calls \"what all people nowadays report and believe,\" while Gibbon judged this unreliable and proposed sabotage or accident; divine intervention remains the common view among Christian historians."
