  {
   "url": "https://www.ebsco.com/research-starters/literature-and-writing/julian-gore-vidal",
   "title": "Julian by Gore Vidal | Literature and Writing | Research Starters | EBSCO Research",
   "snippet": "Set in the aftermath of Julian&#x27;s death in 363 CE, the narrative unfolds through the perspectives of his contemporaries, philosophers <strong>Libanius and Priscus</strong>, who seek to publish Julian&#x27;s memoir to counter the rise of Christianity under Emperor ...",
   "text": "This EBSCO Research Starter, written by William E. Pemberton and published 2021, is excerpted from Magill's Survey of American Literature, Revised Edition; it summarizes Vidal's novel Julian (first published 1964), his first venture into historical fiction. Most of the page is plot summary, which is dropped here; what follows is what the page says about Vidal's method, his views, and the reception testimony it records. The article states that Vidal had read as widely in history as most professional historians, that he adhered closely to the historical record while exercising a novelist's license to invent facts where facts are unknown and to ascribe motives, choosing inventions that seemed most likely to have been true historically, and that he intended both to entertain and to instruct. The article attributes several interpretive beliefs to Vidal: that Julian was murdered by one of his own Christian soldiers during the Persian campaign; that Julian was captivated by Alexander the Great's vision of conquering the known world; that the era was crucial to the evolution of the West; and that if Julian had lived, Christianity would be only one of several religions in Western civilization. It also records, as testimony about the work rather than the man, that one of Vidal's biographers (the article does not name which) asked two outstanding historians of the fourth-century Roman Empire to evaluate the novel, and that the historians agreed Vidal's work is probably the best portrait of Julian that exists. The page quotes one line verbatim from the novel itself, spoken by the character Libanius after Julian's death: \"With Julian, the light went, and now nothing remains but to let the darkness come.\" The article notes the novel's frame structure — Julian's memoir interspersed with letters and comments by Libanius of Antioch and Priscus of Athens, presented as eyewitnesses who correct his account — and dates the frame narrative to 380 C.E., seventeen years after Julian's death in 363 during the Persian invasion, with Julian born about 331, placed in control of the provinces beyond the Alps in 355 C.E., and becoming emperor in 361 on Constantius's death."
  },
  {
   "url": "https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2013/2013.04.24/",
   "title": "Emperor and Author: The Writings of Julian the Apostate – Bryn Mawr Classical Review",
   "snippet": "Nicholas Baker-Brian, Shaun Tougher, ... the Apostate. Swansea: Classical Press of Wales, 2012. xxi, 384. ISBN 9781905125500. $110.00. ... Does it take one author to know another? Perhaps like Gore Vidal, Julian would have entitled his memoirs a palimpsest. Both men grew up in a “house of Atreus” and both ultimately proved to be failed politicians, one to act as an emperor by right of birth, the other to constitute himself as a shadow ...",
   "text": "Hagith Sivan's review (BMCR 2013.04.24) of Baker-Brian and Tougher (eds.), Emperor and Author: The Writings of Julian the Apostate (Classical Press of Wales, 2012), is a summary of nineteen modern scholarly essays, not testimony from anyone who knew Julian; its value here is limited to the specifics it relays about Julian's own writings and acts. The review reports that Mark Humphries' contribution treats Julian's letter to the Athenians, dispatched from Naissus when civil war with Constantius was looming, as a rare document outlining a justification for usurpation, and notes that similar messages were sent to other cities, an act the review calls one of unparalleled originality; Humphries, as summarized, traces how Julian recast Constantius from the benevolent ruler of his earlier panegyrics into a tyrant who ought to be deposed, and the review adds that the letter's efficacy was never tested. Jill Harries' essay, per the review, asks which of Julian's legal rulings are likely his authentic words, finds that the editors of the Theodosian Code ignored Julian's intentions while incorporating his rules, and concludes that Julian's laws reflected his personality and style more than those of any imperial successor; the review notes Harries' observation that as a member of Constantine's family Julian could not ignore predecessors' laws but could engage in covert criticism. Liz James, as the review summarizes, examines Julian's speech of thanks to Eusebia, Constantius' wife and Julian's patron at court, noting it is the only surviving panegyric about a woman, and floats possibilities the review reports with hedges — that the speech implicitly criticizes Eusebia, that it presumes an audience's complicit silence, or that it was perhaps written retrospectively after the parties were dead. Liebeschuetz's essay, the review reports, reads the Hymn to the Mother of the Gods as a centrepiece of Julian's religious revival, and the review quotes the hymn's closing prayer to Cybele in translation: \"Grant me that I may have true knowledge… and that the close of my life may be painless and glorious\" — to which the reviewer adds the skeptical aside, \"Was it?\" The review also relays Salway's conclusion that Julian's genuine voice rarely comes through in inscriptions, and López Sánchez's finding that Julian's coinage, including the Arles mint and the bull coinage, conformed to established imperial patterns rather than personal preference."
  },
  {
   "url": "https://edinburghuniversitypress.com/book-julian-the-apostate.html",
   "title": "Julian the Apostate",
   "snippet": "<strong>This study of the last pagan Roman emperor provides remarkable insight into the man and his times</strong>The figure of Julian demands the attention of historians. As the last pagan Roman Emperor, he provides a focus for studying the religious ...",
   "text": "This page is the Edinburgh University Press catalogue entry for Shaun Tougher's 'Julian the Apostate' (paperback published January 2007, 224 pages, in the Debates and Documents in Ancient History series; ISBN paperback 9780748618873), and it is publisher marketing copy rather than testimony — nothing on it records Julian's own words or a contemporary's account directly. The publisher's description states that Julian, the last pagan Roman emperor, elicited sharply divided opinion from his contemporaries, polarised largely between pagan supporters and Christian opponents, and that modern literature on him is similarly divided; the page poses the book's guiding questions, including whether Julian was the prospective saviour of the empire or out of touch, whether he was an evangelist for Mithraism or a more traditional pagan, and whether he was a shrewd military man or a rash risk-taker whose luck ran out on the Persian expedition. The contents listing is the page's main evidentiary value for this slot, since it enumerates the primary testimonies the volume gathers: Part II documents include Julian's own writings (the Panegyrics on Constantius and on Eusebia, On the Deeds of the Emperor Constantius or On Kingship, the Consolation on the departure of Salutius, letters to Priscus, Oribasius, the Athenians, Maximus, Themistius, Bishop Aetius, the people of Alexandria, Ecdicus, Evagrius, the Thracians, the high-priest Theodorus, Arsacius of Galatia, Atarbius, and the citizens of Bostra, the Rescript on Christian Teachers, the edict to the Alexandrians, Against the Cynic Heraclius, To the Mother of the Gods, The Caesars, To King Helios, Letter to a Priest, and the Misopogon), plus inscriptions, the Theodosian Code, and testimony from others — Sextus Aurelius Victor's De Caesaribus, Claudius Mamertinus's Speech of Thanks to Julian, eight Libanius items including the Funeral Oration for Julian, Upon Avenging Julian, and the Autobiography, Salutius's Concerning the Gods and the Universe, and a Gregory of Nazianzus item whose title is cut off on the page. Part I comprises thematic chapters: Family, Conversion, Gaul, Emperor: Style and Reform, Religion, and Persia, framed by an introduction on 'The Fascination of Julian' and a conclusion on 'The Elusiveness of Julian'. The author note identifies Tougher as Professor of Late Roman and Byzantine History at Cardiff University, with a BA from Queen's University Belfast and a PhD from St Andrews, working on dynastic history and on gender and society, particularly eunuchs."
  },
  {
   "url": "https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2007/2007.09.36",
   "title": "Julian the Apostate. Debates and Documents in Ancient History series – Bryn Mawr Classical Review",
   "snippet": "3). Chapters 4 and 5, while still in line with the general chronology, are more thematic in character: they discuss the ‘imperial style’ of Julian (Ch. 4) and his religion (Ch. 5). Accompanying the volume’s historical overview of Julian are a timeline, a family tree of the emperor, and three maps of the Empire; a guided ‘further reading’ section, a section with ‘essay questions and exercise topics’, and a list of Internet resources.",
   "text": "Sergio Knipe of the University of Cambridge, writing in BMCR 2007.09.36, reviews Shaun Tougher’s 2007 *Julian the Apostate*, a 201-page introduction combining historical debate with translated source extracts. Knipe’s contribution is bibliographic and critical rather than eyewitness testimony: he records that Tougher’s documentary section includes material attributed to Julian himself, Libanius, Eunapius, Ammianus, Zosimus, Eutropius and Festus; Mamertinus’ panegyric of 362; excerpts from the *Theodosian Code*; four inscriptions; passages from Christian authors; three coin types; and the Louvre statue of Julian. Knipe notes the omission of Julian’s *Against the Uneducated Cynics* and of the Ma‘ayan Barukh inscription praising Julian as *restaurator templorum*. He also reports that the documents mostly reuse standard English translations, with only a few adaptations and Orosius the sole author freshly translated; the translations used for Socrates and Philostorgius date respectively to 1890 and 1855.\n\nOn contested evidence, Knipe says Tougher follows R. Asmus in treating Julian’s letters to priests as an attempt to instruct the clergy of a newly organized pagan church, but Mario Mazza considers their purpose and the character of Julian’s religious reform much less clear. Knipe also cites Peter van Nuffelen’s argument that the so-called *Letter to Arsacius* might be a fifth-century fabrication. Elsewhere, Tougher reportedly presents Julian’s accession as possibly resulting from a pagan conspiracy, without engaging Jean Bouffartigue’s contrary discussion. The page preserves no direct words of Julian and no testimony from anyone who knew him."
  }
