 "conflicts": [
  {
   "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Against_the_Galileans",
   "title": "Against the Galileans - Wikipedia",
   "snippet": "Against the Galileans (Ancient Greek: Κατὰ Γαλιλαίων; Latin: Contra Galilaeos), meaning Christians, was a Greek polemical essay – now mostly lost – written by the Roman emperor <strong>Julian</strong>, commonly known as <strong>Julian</strong> <strong>the</strong> <strong>Apostate</strong>, during his short reign (361–363).",
   "text": "The Wikipedia article on Against the Galilaeans (Greek Kata Galilaiōn, Latin Contra Galilaeos) describes it as a Greek polemical essay, now mostly lost, written by the emperor Julian during his reign of 361–363, directed against the Christians, whom he called 'Galileans.' The article reports that Julian, a nephew of Constantine raised as a Christian, studied with Neoplatonists and secretly abandoned Christianity in 351. As emperor he legalized cult sacrifice, restored many pagan temples, and financed cults, seeking to end the preceding decades' persecution of pagans; the article states that he did not persecute Christians or forbid Christianity, but ended state subsidies to the church and ended the punishments previously imposed on heretical Christians. He also wrote treatises attacking positions he opposed, including two against those he called 'false Cynics.' Against the Galileans was composed during his stay in Antioch in the winter of 362–363.\n\nThe article reports that the text does not survive, probably destroyed under an anti-pagan edict in 448 or 529, and that knowledge of it comes second-hand, mainly from Cyril of Alexandria's refutation Contra Julianum, which quoted it at length and was finished either between 434–437 or 439–441. Only ten books of Cyril's refutation survive intact, all covering the first of Julian's three books, with fragments of another ten books surviving. Cyril claimed it was among the most important anti-Christian works written and was widely considered irrefutable; Libanius praised it as surpassing even Porphyry of Tyre's critiques. Before Cyril, only Theodore of Mopsuestia had attempted a refutation, though many Christian authors had written scornfully of Julian. The article highlights the conflict between Julian and Cyril over Judaism: Julian supported the Jewish community and sought to rebuild the Temple in Jerusalem, while Cyril wrote that Jews stood in the way of Christianity and that Gentiles should reject all things Jewish, including rebuilding the Temple. The article suggests that perhaps this disagreement made Cyril's refutation so bitter — Cyril portrays Julian as satanically inspired and the Greek tradition as folly, arguing that whatever truth the Greeks had came from Moses, whom even Plato supposedly admired.\n\nThe article's synopsis of the surviving fragments, with section numbers preserved, runs as follows. Julian criticizes the Galileans for denying the gods and for laziness and superstition taken from the Greeks, arguing that all men innately know God exists and believe the gods dwell in the heavens, eternal and unchanging (43A–52C). He ridicules literal readings of Genesis: how could the serpent speak a human language (86A); a true God would not withhold knowledge of good and evil or be jealous of men eating of the tree of life, so the God depicted appears evil and the serpent, giving the gift of distinguishing good from evil, appears good, meaning the account must have a deeper sense (75B, 89B, 94A). He asks where the abyss, darkness, and waters came from, and the angels, who are absent from the creation account (49C); Genesis, he argues, describes not a creator God but an inferior god shaping pre-existing matter, comparable to Plato's Timaeus (49E, 58C). There must be multiple shaping gods, since one God would produce identical beings, whereas immortals, humans, and animals differ (65D, 66A). The God of Moses, who chose only the Jews and gave them alone prophecy, is merely the national god of the Jews; Julian finds it absurd that the creator of everything, self-described as jealous, confined his care to one small Palestinian tribe for thousands of years while all other races worshipped false gods (106D–E). The Greeks, he says, rightly treat the gods as delegates of the creator God responsible for different nations, explaining differences in character and custom (115D, 131C); he mocks the Tower of Babel story as unable to explain differences in morals, laws, or physique (138A), attributing these to different national gods (143A). The God of Moses exists but is inferior to the God of All (148B); if he were the only god, why have the Jews achieved less than Greeks, Phoenicians, or Egyptians, and why have they been so often subjugated (178A, 213A)? Julian then argues the Galileans, despite nominally embracing Jewish tradition, rejected it while keeping only its blasphemy of the gods (238A–B): Jesus cannot be the prophet foretold by Moses and Isaiah, since as son of God he was not descended from Judah, and a sole God creating an equal second God violates Jewish monotheism (253A–B). Christians abandoned animal sacrifice (305D), dietary laws (314C), and the law Moses warned against changing (320B), and even the apostles' teaching — no apostle called Jesus God until John, who, Julian claims, probably did so only to settle a dispute in a divided church (327A). Christians revere tombs though Jesus called gravesites impure (335B), and abandoned circumcision and Passover despite their importance in Jewish law (351A); the practices of Moses and Abraham, Julian claims, are closer to those of the Greeks than to those of the Galileans (356C). The article cites Smith 1995 (Julian's Gods) and Russell 2000 (Cyril of Alexandria) throughout, and notes English translations by Wilmer Cave Wright (1923) and C.W. King (1888)."
  },
  {
   "url": "https://fightingthegods.com/against-the-galileans-julian-on-the-contradiction-at-the-heart-of-scripture/",
   "title": "Against the Galileans: Julian on the Contradiction at the Heart of Scripture - Fighting the Gods",
   "snippet": "One abstains from pork, the other eats it, and neither can establish, beyond reasonable doubt, which position is “correct.” So, they condemn each other, selectively cite scripture to suit their purpose, and, when they attain political power, impose their beliefs upon “nonbelievers” and “apostates” with the full force of the almighty God behind them. The result is the long, often bloody history of Western monotheism that Julian heroically tried, yet failed, to stop.",
   "text": "This page from the blog Fighting the Gods (an avowedly anti-Christian site, so its framing is polemical; it is summarising and editorialising rather than recording primary testimony) presents the Emperor Julian (331–363 CE) as a pagan critic of Christianity whose conflict with the faith was intellectual and political rather than violent. The page reports that Julian, raised Christian but educated in Greek classics, wrote Against the Galileans to argue Christians had apostasised twice — from the Greek gods and from the Jewish law they claimed to inherit. It notes the work barely survives: the page reports that Theodosius II in 448 CE ordered non-orthodox works burned, leaving perhaps 15–20 percent of Julian's text, preserved mainly through quotations in Cyril of Alexandria's hostile refutation. On conduct, the page reports that Julian's roughly 20-month reign was 'overwhelmingly peaceful' toward Christians — he reopened temples, recalled exiled bishops of all sects, and (I am paraphrasing, per my constraints) described his own policy as persuading by reason rather than blows or violence, and claimed no Galilean had suffered violence or forced temple attendance under him — while hedging that his toleration was imperfect, citing his 'school edict' that effectively barred Christians from teaching. He died on campaign at 32. The page also paraphrases (as I do in turn) Julian advocating sharing money, clothes, and food even with the wicked, and likens his virtue-driven politics to Marcus Aurelius. The core conflict the page extracts from the fragments: Julian argued Christianity is logically incoherent because Jesus affirmed the Mosaic law as eternal (the page cites Matt. 5:17, 5:19 and Deut. 29:29, with Julian claiming Moses calls the law 'eternal' in countless passages such as Exodus 12:14, 12:17, 31:16-17) yet himself dissolved it — over the Sabbath (Mark 2:27-28), dietary law (Mark 7:18-19 and Matt. 15 against Leviticus 11; the page notes John the Baptist's locust-eating at Mark 1:6 as taking the law seriously), and divorce (Deut. 24:1-2 permitting it, Luke 16:18 and Matt. 19:7-8 forbidding it, with Jesus attributing Moses's permission to hardness of heart). The page flags one hedge of its own: it brackets the scholarly consensus that Moses did not write the Pentateuch. Julian also attacked Judaism's portrait of God: the page reports he asked what nation does not already observe most of the Ten Commandments apart from the monotheism and Sabbath commands; mocked God's jealousy and punishment of children for parents' sins (Exod. 20:1-6) as anthropomorphic; and cited the Phinehas episode (Num. 25), asking why the Hebrew God swings from rage to satisfaction at a bloody double killing, calling such vacillation unfitting for a universal God, and contrasting it with Epicurean tranquility. Finally, the page attributes to Julian three foreseen dangers of Christianity: its uncompromising monotheism hostile to religious pluralism, its tendency to suppress rather than debate pagan literature, and its internal contradictions breeding schism and, once politically empowered, coercive violence. My confidence is moderate that these paraphrases track Julian's genuine surviving fragments (the school edict, recall of bishops, and Cyril-mediated transmission are well attested), but the page's exact wording of Julian's quotations cannot be verified from memory and some renderings (e.g., 'Master' for the divine name in the Exodus passage) look like the site's own translation choices."
